    "marked": false,
    "answer": "<p>The best solution is to use <b>Leverage Amazon Athena to directly query the JSON logs housed in S3 as needed<\/b>. Athena is a serverless service, so there's no infrastructure to manage, and you can easily run SQL queries directly on data in Amazon S3. No data movement or transformation is initially required, and operational overhead is minimal, which perfectly matches the requirements.<\/p><p>Here's why the other options are not optimal:<\/p><ul><li><i>Import the log data into Amazon Redshift and use SQL statements for querying<\/i>: This would require moving data into Redshift and possibly transforming formats, increasing both time and operational complexity.<\/li><li><i>Catalog the logs with AWS Glue, and perform on-demand SQL analysis via a temporary Apache Spark cluster on Amazon EMR<\/i>: While this is technically viable, it involves more moving pieces (Glue, EMR) and is better suited for more complex ETL or advanced analytics requirements. The operational overhead is higher compared to Athena.<\/li><li><i>Store the log files in Amazon CloudWatch Logs and use the CloudWatch console to run SQL queries as required<\/i>: CloudWatch Logs does not natively support full SQL querying over logs, making this option impractical for arbitrary queries, especially on structured JSON data.<\/li><\/ul><p><strong>Tip:<\/strong> Athena integrates seamlessly with AWS Glue Catalog if you want to manage tables centrally, but it's optional for simple queries. Athena pricing is based on the amount of data scanned, so optimize your queries with partitions and select only the necessary columns.<\/p>",
