    "id": 5,
    "query": "A small digital marketing agency runs its traffic analytics dashboard on a single Amazon EC2 On-Demand instance. The dashboard, developed in PHP, includes a built-in MySQL database and web server, all running on the same virtual machine. During periods of high client activity, the dashboard begins to return <strong>5xx errors</strong>, and users experience slow response times. The agency is looking for a <strong>cost-effective</strong> way to enhance <strong>scalability</strong> and reduce <strong>downtime</strong>, especially during traffic surges. <br><br><strong>Which solution offers the most scalable and budget-conscious architecture?</strong>",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Option 4</strong></p><p>Option 4 is the best and most cost-effective solution. Migrating the database to Amazon Aurora MySQL offloads database load from the EC2 instance and provides scalability, high availability, and performance improvements. Creating an AMI and using it in a launch template allows for consistent deployment of your application. Configuring an Auto Scaling group with Spot Instances helps minimize cost while allowing the application to scale out during peak traffic. Attaching an Application Load Balancer ensures traffic is routed to healthy instances, enabling seamless horizontal scaling and improved availability.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 1:</strong> Functional but less cost-effective due to the use of On-Demand EC2 instances and lack of dynamic scaling.</li><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> Route 53 weighted routing lacks health checks and scaling flexibility compared to an ALB. It doesn't adapt automatically to changes in traffic.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> Using a Lambda to resize an EC2 instance introduces downtime, doesn't offer high availability, and doesn\u2019t support horizontal scaling.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question is challenging:</strong> It tests knowledge of AWS scaling strategies, EC2 cost models, database decoupling, and elasticity using Auto Scaling groups and Spot Instances.</p>",
    "marked": 0,
    "timespent": 0,
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Migrate the MySQL database to Amazon RDS for MySQL. Create a machine image (AMI) of the PHP application. Use the AMI to spin up multiple EC2 instances as needed. Set up an Application Load Balancer to evenly distribute incoming traffic across the instances.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Migrate the database to Amazon RDS for MySQL. Create an AMI of the PHP web server and deploy it on a second EC2 On-Demand instance. Use Amazon Route 53 weighted routing to split traffic across both instances.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Migrate the database to Amazon Aurora MySQL. Create an AWS Lambda function that stops the EC2 instance and modifies its instance type. Trigger the function using an Amazon CloudWatch alarm when CPU usage exceeds 75%.",
