  {
    "id": 6,
    "query": "A financial software firm operates a <strong>stateless customer-facing application</strong> hosted on Amazon EC2 instances. The workload sits behind an <strong>Application Load Balancer</strong> and scales with demand. During regular business hours, the application sees a predictable spike in traffic lasting around 8 hours each weekday. Overnight, traffic remains steady but moderate, while on weekends, usage drops to minimal levels. The firm wants to <strong>optimize cost efficiency</strong> without compromising <strong>application availability</strong>. <br><br><strong>What is the most effective solution?</strong>",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Option 1</strong></p><p>Option 1 is the most cost-effective and appropriate solution. By purchasing Reserved Instances, the company secures a lower, predictable cost for the always-on, baseline level of usage, especially overnight and moderate weekend traffic. For the predictable business-day spikes, Spot Instances are ideal for handling the additional compute load at a significantly reduced price. Because the application is stateless and behind a load balancer, instances can be added or removed dynamically without impacting user experience.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> Too risky\u2014Spot Instances can be interrupted at any time, leading to potential downtime during critical hours.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> Functional but more expensive than using Reserved Instances for baseline usage.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> Dedicated Instances are costlier and unnecessary unless compliance or licensing isolation is required.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question is challenging:</strong> It evaluates understanding of EC2 pricing models, demand patterns, and combining instance types (Reserved + Spot) to achieve cost efficiency and high availability.</p>",
    "marked": 0,
    "timespent": 0,
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Purchase Reserved Instances to cover the baseline demand. Use Spot Instances to handle peak usage during business hours.",
        "correct": true,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Run the entire workload using Spot Instances to reduce compute costs as much as possible.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Run baseline traffic on On-Demand Instances. Add Spot Instances during high-demand periods to absorb excess traffic.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Use Dedicated Instances to handle the constant baseline demand and supplement with On-Demand Instances when more capacity is needed.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      }
    ],
    "objectives": []
  },
  {
    "id": 8,
    "query": "A healthcare analytics company generates over <strong>8 terabytes</strong> of diagnostic log data every month from its machine learning systems. Industry regulations require the company to retain these logs for at least <strong>7 years</strong>. The operations team regularly examines logs from the last <strong>45 days</strong> for model debugging and issue resolution, but data older than that is rarely accessed. The company wants a <strong>cost-effective</strong> solution that allows fast access to recent logs while ensuring secure, long-term retention for compliance purposes. What is the best approach to meet these needs?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Option 1</strong></p><p>Option 1 is the correct and most cost-effective solution. By storing logs in Amazon S3, the company gains access to reliable and durable storage with native support for lifecycle policies. These policies can automatically transition older logs to S3 Glacier Deep Archive, which is ideal for long-term storage at a fraction of the cost of standard S3. Keeping recent logs in the standard storage tier allows for fast access while meeting compliance obligations through automatic archival.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> AWS Backup does not manage S3 transitions. S3 Lifecycle handles transitions natively.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> CloudWatch Logs is expensive at scale and exporting adds complexity.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> Lifecycle policies cannot be applied directly to CloudWatch Logs.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question is challenging:</strong> It assesses your knowledge of data retention strategies and lifecycle policy configurations.</p>",
    "marked": 0,
    "timespent": 0,
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Store all logs in Amazon S3 and configure an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition logs older than 45 days to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.",
        "correct": true,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Store all logs in Amazon S3 and use AWS Backup to move logs older than 45 days to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Send logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. Use AWS Backup to export logs older than 45 days to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Send logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. Use Amazon S3 Lifecycle policies to transition logs older than 45 days to S3 Glacier Deep Archive.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      }
    ],
    "objectives": []
  },
  {
    "id": 9,
    "query": "A logistics company operates a real-time parcel tracking pipeline using AWS services. The system is triggered by events published to an <strong>Amazon SNS</strong> topic when new tracking data arrives. An <strong>AWS Lambda</strong> function subscribes to this topic and processes each event, enriching the data and storing it in a database. Occasionally, processing fails due to transient network disruptions, causing data to be lost unless manually reprocessed. The company wants a resilient, automated way to ensure that no tracking event is permanently lost. What should a solutions architect recommend?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Option 1</strong></p><p>Option 1 is the correct and most reliable solution. By introducing an SQS queue between SNS and Lambda, or as a dead-letter queue (DLQ) for Lambda, the company gains a durable buffer that holds all failed events. Even if the Lambda invocation fails due to a network issue or temporary outage, the message will persist in the queue until it is successfully processed. This ensures no data is lost and manual intervention is minimized.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> Extending retry policies doesn\u2019t ensure delivery if all retries fail.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> Increasing memory/CPU doesn\u2019t fix external network failures or introduce durability.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> Lambda is already AZ-resilient; manual deployment across AZs is unnecessary and unsupported.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question is challenging:</strong> It tests your understanding of event durability, retry strategies, and the differences between SNS and SQS.</p>",
    "marked": 0,
    "timespent": 0,
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Set up an Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue as a dead-letter destination for failed invocations. Update the Lambda function to poll and process messages from the queue.",
        "correct": true,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Adjust the SNS topic\u2019s retry policy to increase the number of delivery attempts and extend the delay between retries.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Configure the Lambda function to use more memory and CPU to reduce the likelihood of processing timeouts and improve reliability.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Deploy the Lambda function redundantly across multiple AWS Availability Zones to improve its availability and reduce the chance of failure.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      }
    ],
    "objectives": []
  },
  {
    "id": 10,
    "query": "A media streaming platform has a component that generates a continuous stream of user interaction events. These events must be processed in the <strong>exact sequence</strong> they are received to preserve session integrity and ensure accurate analytics. The company prefers a solution that requires <strong>minimal maintenance</strong> and <strong>scales automatically</strong>. What is the best AWS-native approach to meet these requirements?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Option 1</strong></p><p>Option 1 is the correct and most appropriate choice. Amazon SQS FIFO (First-In-First-Out) queues are specifically designed to preserve the exact order of message delivery, which is critical in this scenario. When paired with a Lambda function, this setup allows for event-driven processing while minimizing operational overhead. The FIFO queue ensures strict ordering, and Lambda scales automatically with the incoming workload, satisfying both the ordering and low-maintenance requirements.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> SNS does not guarantee message order and lacks durable retry logic for failed Lambda executions.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> SQS standard queues do not preserve message order and may deliver messages out of sequence.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> SNS cannot publish to FIFO queues, and the combination does not guarantee message ordering.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question is challenging:</strong> It tests knowledge of message ordering, service integration, and managed event-driven architecture.</p>",
    "marked": 0,
    "timespent": 0,
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Use an Amazon SQS FIFO queue to store incoming events. Configure an AWS Lambda function to poll and process the messages in order.",
        "correct": true,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Publish each event to an Amazon SNS topic that delivers payloads to a Lambda function for processing.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Use an Amazon SQS standard queue to hold event messages. Trigger a Lambda function to process messages concurrently as they arrive.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Send event data to an SNS topic with an Amazon SQS queue subscribed to it. Process the queue messages with a Lambda function.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      }
    ],
    "objectives": []
  },
  {
    "id": 11,
    "query": "An educational technology company is transitioning a legacy grading system from its internal data center to Amazon EC2 instances. As part of the migration, the cloud architecture team must implement infrastructure monitoring. One requirement is to avoid reacting to brief CPU usage spikes above 50%, which are common during batch uploads. However, if the CPU usage exceeds 50% and disk read IOPS are simultaneously high, the team must be alerted immediately to prevent performance degradation. Reducing false alarms is a priority. What is the most effective way to meet these needs?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Option 1</strong></p><p>Option 1 is the correct choice. Amazon CloudWatch composite alarms allow you to combine multiple metric alarms into a single evaluation. This enables an alarm to trigger only when both conditions\u2014high CPU utilization and elevated read IOPS\u2014are met simultaneously. This approach significantly reduces noise from transient CPU spikes and ensures that alerts are only raised for meaningful performance concerns, precisely meeting the requirements.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> Dashboards do not trigger alarms and rely on manual monitoring.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> CloudWatch Synthetics is not used for infrastructure metrics like CPU or IOPS.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> Metric alarms evaluate only one metric at a time and cannot combine distinct metrics without composite alarms.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question is challenging:</strong> It tests understanding of CloudWatch alarm types and how to configure them to reduce false positives while maintaining visibility into true issues.</p>",
    "marked": 0,
    "timespent": 0,
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Use Amazon CloudWatch composite alarms to trigger alerts only when both CPU utilization and read IOPS exceed defined thresholds.",
        "correct": true,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Build Amazon CloudWatch dashboards that display both CPU and IOPS metrics for real-time visibility and faster manual response.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Deploy Amazon CloudWatch Synthetics canaries to simulate end-user actions and alert on performance degradation.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      },
      {
        "text": "Create individual Amazon CloudWatch metric alarms with multiple threshold conditions for each relevant metric.",
        "correct": false,
        "selected": false
      }
    ],
    "objectives": []
  },
