    "id": 13,
    "query": "An HR consulting firm runs a web-based onboarding platform that delivers orientation material to new hires. The application follows a standard three-tier architecture, with a web frontend, application logic, and a backend database hosted on <strong>Amazon RDS for MySQL</strong>. Employees access the system for only 9 hours each day, and the company wants to reduce database-related costs without affecting functionality. What is the best solution to meet these needs?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Option 1</strong></p><p>Using AWS Lambda functions triggered by Amazon EventBridge scheduled rules is a clean, serverless, and cost-effective way to manage scheduled operations like starting and stopping an RDS DB instance. This minimizes operational overhead and ensures the database is only running during necessary hours, significantly reducing cost.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> Session Manager and IAM policies are not built for scheduled DB start/stop automation.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> ElastiCache cannot replace a relational database while the database is offline.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> Using EC2 for cron jobs is outdated, costly, and operationally heavy compared to Lambda.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question is challenging:</strong> It evaluates your ability to use serverless automation to reduce costs while maintaining system functionality.</p>",
    "marked": 0,
    "timespent": 0,
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "Create AWS Lambda functions to start and stop the RDS DB instance. Use Amazon EventBridge (formerly CloudWatch Events) to schedule the functions based on the required operating hours. Assign the functions as event targets.",
