    "id": 16,
    "query": "A global entertainment company operates a web application hosted on AWS that serves premium multimedia content to subscribers. The media files are stored in Amazon S3, and some of the content is sensitive and must remain secure. The company wants to cache these files to improve performance and ensure users worldwide can access them quickly and reliably, regardless of their geographic location. What is the best solution to meet these requirements?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Option 1</strong></p><p>Amazon CloudFront is a global content delivery network (CDN) that caches content at edge locations worldwide. Integrating it with S3 allows fast, secure, and scalable content delivery. CloudFront also supports signed URLs and HTTPS encryption for secure distribution of sensitive content.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> AWS DataSync is meant for bulk transfers, not real-time caching for global performance.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> Global Accelerator improves TCP/UDP app latency but doesn't cache content or integrate with S3 directly.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> Amazon SQS is a queuing service and unrelated to content delivery or caching.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question is challenging:</strong> It requires knowing which AWS service functions as a CDN and how to securely deliver static content to a global audience.</p>",
    "marked": 0,
    "timespent": 0,
