    "id": 17,
    "query": "A technology company collects a mix of batch data from various databases and real-time streaming data from application APIs and network sensors. The business needs to centralize all this data into a single location for future analytics. The data must be processed and then stored in separate Amazon S3 buckets, organized for different business teams. Later, analysts will run one-time queries and visualize key performance indicators (KPIs) using a business intelligence tool. The company wants a solution with minimal operational overhead and strong compatibility with AWS-native analytics tools. Which combination of steps best fulfills these requirements?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answers: Option 1 and Option 5</strong></p><p><strong>Option 1:</strong> Amazon Athena allows one-time queries on data in S3 using standard SQL syntax, without provisioning infrastructure. Amazon QuickSight integrates with Athena to generate visual dashboards for KPIs.</p><p><strong>Option 5:</strong> AWS Lake Formation automates the creation of a centralized data lake, and AWS Glue can extract, transform, and load structured and unstructured data into S3. Storing in Apache Parquet optimizes data for performance and storage cost.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> Kinesis Data Analytics is built for real-time continuous analysis, not ad hoc or batch queries.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> Custom Lambda and Redshift introduces more maintenance and is overkill for infrequent querying.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> OpenSearch is great for logs and text search, not structured business analytics or BI dashboarding.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question is challenging:</strong> It evaluates your knowledge of data lakes, analytics pipelines, real-time vs. batch processing, and AWS-native analytics tools like Athena and QuickSight.</p>",
