    "query": "A financial analytics company stores transactional data in an Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL DB cluster. By policy, the company must retain all transactional data for exactly 4 years and then permanently delete it. However, audit logs that track all activity within the database must be preserved indefinitely. Currently, the Aurora cluster is configured with automated backups. What combination of actions should a solutions architect recommend to meet these regulatory and operational requirements?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answers: Option 1 and Option 4</strong></p><p><strong>Option 1:</strong> AWS Backup allows centralized backup management for Aurora, including long-term retention and scheduled deletion after 4 years.</p><p><strong>Option 4:</strong> Exporting Aurora audit logs to CloudWatch enables long-term, independent retention for auditing and compliance.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> Manual snapshots don\u2019t support lifecycle enforcement or automation.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> Aurora automated backup retention maxes out at 35 days.</li><li><strong>Option 5:</strong> Lifecycle policies don\u2019t apply to Aurora backups\u2014only to S3 objects.</li></ul>",
