    "id": 21,
    "query": "A fintech company operates a serverless web API that is publicly accessible and built using Amazon API Gateway with AWS Lambda on the backend. Recently, the company observed a sharp increase in traffic caused by automated botnets and fraudulent requests, which are disrupting performance and incurring additional costs. A solutions architect has been asked to implement measures to prevent unauthorized access while still allowing legitimate users to use the service without friction. What combination of actions should the architect take to address this problem effectively?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answers: Option 1 and Option 3</strong></p><p><strong>Option 1:</strong> Requiring an API key with usage plans helps control and filter access to your API. Keys issued only to trusted users make it harder for bots to abuse the system.</p><p><strong>Option 3:</strong> AWS WAF protects API Gateway by allowing you to define rules that block malicious traffic, including IP-based and pattern-based blocking, effectively mitigating botnets.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> Blocking requests within Lambda is inefficient and incurs costs before filtering is applied.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> A private API would block all public access, which contradicts the original use case.</li><li><strong>Option 5:</strong> IAM roles are impractical for public access APIs with broad user bases.</li></ul>",
    "marked": 0,
    "timespent": 0,
