    "query": "A financial services firm is migrating its high performance computing (HPC) workloads to AWS for advanced risk modeling. These workloads are Linux-based, run on hundreds of EC2 Spot Instances, and generate large volumes of output files that must be retained for long-term analytics. The company also wants to copy some datasets from its on-premises storage into the cloud and make the data accessible to all compute nodes during processing. The cloud storage solution must deliver high-throughput, low-latency performance, and must be able to integrate with persistent storage for final data archiving. What AWS solution best meets these requirements?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Amazon FSx for Lustre integrated with Amazon S3</strong></p><p>FSx for Lustre is optimized for Linux-based HPC workloads and offers sub-millisecond latency, parallel I/O, and high throughput\u2014ideal for EC2 Spot fleets. It integrates directly with Amazon S3, enabling data import and export for cost-effective long-term storage. This solution provides both performance during compute and persistent storage for archiving.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> FSx for Windows File Server is built for SMB-based Windows workloads, not HPC on Linux.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> S3 Glacier is archival storage with slow retrieval\u2014unsuitable for real-time processing.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> S3 with VPC endpoints and EBS does not provide a shared, distributed file system required for HPC workflows.</li></ul>",
