    "query": "A software company has developed a containerized application on premises and is planning to migrate it to AWS. The application is expected to gain thousands of users shortly after launch. The company wants to deploy the app in a highly available architecture that can scale efficiently with demand while requiring minimal operational management. The team is looking for a solution that avoids managing the underlying infrastructure. Which AWS approach best meets these requirements?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Option 1</strong></p><p>AWS Fargate with Amazon ECS is the optimal solution for running containers without managing servers. By using Amazon ECR to store images and ECS with the Fargate launch type, the company can run containers in a serverless environment that scales automatically. Target tracking ensures efficient scaling based on demand. This approach maximizes availability and minimizes operational overhead.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Option 2:</strong> ECS with EC2 requires you to manage and scale EC2 instances, adding operational complexity.</li><li><strong>Option 3:</strong> Hosting containers directly on EC2 introduces scaling and fault tolerance challenges.</li><li><strong>Option 4:</strong> Baking containers into AMIs is inflexible and lacks orchestration features like ECS or EKS.</li></ul>",
