    "id": 26,
    "query": "A financial analytics firm runs two decoupled services. One service generates financial transaction payloads and sends them hourly for processing. The processing service may take up to 48 hours to complete handling each transaction. To ensure resilience, failed transactions must be captured without disrupting the processing of others. The firm needs a solution that requires minimal infrastructure management while remaining highly scalable and fault tolerant. Which solution best addresses these requirements in the most operationally efficient way?",
    "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer: Connect both services to an Amazon Simple Queue Service (SQS) standard queue. Configure a dead-letter queue to handle failed messages for retry or inspection.</strong></p><p>SQS decouples producers and consumers and retains messages for up to 14 days. It handles scaling automatically and is a fully managed service. Dead-letter queues allow failed messages to be retried or inspected without interrupting normal processing. This makes it ideal for applications that have variable processing times and require durable message handling.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Redis on EC2:</strong> Requires manual management and lacks message durability guarantees.</li><li><strong>SNS:</strong> Does not guarantee delivery if the consumer is unavailable and lacks long-term storage.</li><li><strong>Kinesis:</strong> Retention is too short for 48-hour use cases, and adds complexity for simple message queuing.</li></ul>",
    "marked": 0,
    "timespent": 0,
    "options": [
