    "id": 1,
    "query": "An online education platform hosts its static website content, including images, HTML, and CSS files, in an Amazon S3 bucket. To improve performance and enforce security policies, the company wants to distribute this content globally through Amazon CloudFront. However, corporate compliance requires that every request to the site be filtered through AWS WAF to protect against common web exploits. What is the most appropriate way for the solutions architect to design the system while meeting both performance and security requirements?",
    "answer": "Use Amazon CloudFront to serve content from the S3 bucket. Enable AWS WAF on the CloudFront distribution and restrict S3 access using an origin access identity (OAI).<br><br>This is the correct solution because AWS WAF integrates with Amazon CloudFront, not directly with S3. By placing AWS WAF on the CloudFront distribution, all incoming HTTP and HTTPS requests are inspected before reaching the S3 origin. An origin access identity (OAI) is then used to restrict access to the S3 bucket so that only CloudFront can retrieve the content, preventing users from bypassing security controls and directly accessing S3.",
