{
  "id": 9,
  "query": "<p>An insurance company is moving its core MySQL database from an on-premises data center to AWS after a recent outage led to significant disruption in operations. The company’s top priority is to adopt a reliable, fault-tolerant database solution that minimizes the risk of data loss. To meet compliance and availability goals, the company requires a solution that stores every transaction on at least two nodes and ensures that replication is synchronous. What is the best AWS solution that meets these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The best solution is to <strong>create an Amazon RDS MySQL DB instance with Multi-AZ functionality enabled to synchronously replicate the data</strong>. RDS Multi-AZ deployments provide high availability and durability by automatically creating a standby replica in a different Availability Zone. The standby is kept in sync with the primary database using synchronous replication, ensuring that transactions are fully committed on both nodes before completing. This minimizes the chance of data loss in case of a failure.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p>Creating an RDS DB instance with synchronous replication to three nodes in three AZs is not a configuration that exists for Amazon RDS MySQL. That capability is specific to Amazon Aurora, which supports replication to multiple nodes across multiple AZs.</p><p>Creating a read replica in a separate AWS Region introduces asynchronous replication. This means transactions are not guaranteed to be stored in the replica before the write is acknowledged. It is useful for scalability and cross-Region disaster recovery but does not meet the requirement of minimizing data loss with synchronous replication.</p><p>Using an EC2 instance with MySQL and a Lambda function to trigger replication is an unreliable and overly complex architecture. It does not provide the built-in durability or fault tolerance that RDS Multi-AZ offers, and managing consistency through Lambda introduces operational overhead and latency risks.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This scenario tests your understanding of high availability database architectures in AWS, especially when choosing between RDS features, EC2-based databases, and regional replication. Knowing which services offer synchronous replication and fault tolerance is crucial for real-world migration decisions.</p><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> When minimizing data loss is key, look for solutions using synchronous replication. RDS Multi-AZ is often the best choice for MySQL workloads requiring high availability. Be cautious with cross-Region and read replica solutions—they are typically asynchronous unless using Aurora Global Databases.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon RDS DB instance with synchronous replication to three nodes in three Availability Zones.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon RDS MySQL DB instance with Multi-AZ functionality enabled to synchronously replicate the data.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon RDS MySQL DB instance and then create a read replica in a separate AWS Region that synchronously replicates the data.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon EC2 instance with a MySQL engine installed that triggers an AWS Lambda function to synchronously replicate the data to an Amazon RDS MySQL DB instance.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 10,
  "query": "<p>An online food delivery startup is developing a dynamic ordering website to support unpredictable and highly variable traffic, especially during meal-time surges and promotions. The team wants to avoid all server maintenance, and they do not want to perform any database capacity planning. The website must be globally accessible, automatically scale based on demand, and maintain high availability. The company also prefers to pay only for what they use and avoid paying for idle resources. Which architecture best meets these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The best solution is to <strong>host static content in Amazon S3, dynamic content with Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda, use Amazon DynamoDB with on-demand capacity, and deliver content with Amazon CloudFront</strong>. This architecture is entirely serverless, removing the need for provisioning, patching, or scaling infrastructure manually.</p><p><strong>DynamoDB on-demand mode</strong> is the key differentiator here. It provides a fully managed, serverless database experience that automatically adjusts read and write throughput to match traffic levels. It can instantly double capacity compared to the previously observed peak and continues to scale smoothly if traffic continues to grow gradually. Costs are based solely on the number of requests performed, which aligns perfectly with workloads that may be spiky or unpredictable. There's no charge for unused capacity, making this cost-effective for bursty or variable workloads.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p>Using Amazon Aurora with Aurora Auto Scaling still requires some operational overhead for cluster management, monitoring, and scaling configuration. It’s not serverless in the same sense as DynamoDB, and the pay-per-use model is less granular than DynamoDB’s request-based pricing.</p><p>Hosting content entirely on EC2 instances, even with Auto Scaling and an Application Load Balancer, introduces significant operational responsibilities such as OS updates, instance health checks, and scaling logic. This also does not eliminate idle costs, as EC2 instances are billed whether traffic is high or low.</p><p>The EC2 + DynamoDB option is better than Aurora in terms of scaling the database, but EC2 still requires managing infrastructure and doesn't align with the goal of zero server maintenance.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question tests your understanding of AWS serverless architectures and the advanced scaling capabilities of DynamoDB on-demand. Recognizing when DynamoDB’s pricing, latency, and capacity model fits a use case is a key skill for solution architects working with variable or unpredictable workloads.</p><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> When a question emphasizes no server maintenance, automatic scaling, unpredictable workloads, and a cost model that avoids paying for idle resources, a fully serverless solution with <strong>DynamoDB on-demand</strong> is likely the correct choice.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Host static content in Amazon S3. Host dynamic content by using Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda. Use Amazon DynamoDB with on-demand capacity for the database. Configure Amazon CloudFront to deliver the website content.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Host static content in Amazon S3. Host dynamic content by using Amazon API Gateway and AWS Lambda. Use Amazon Aurora with Aurora Auto Scaling for the database. Configure Amazon CloudFront to deliver the website content.",
