{
  "id": 11,
  "query": "<p>A software engineering team at a global enterprise uses a dedicated AWS account that is connected to the company's on-premises data center via two AWS Direct Connect connections. The account is configured so that all non-VPC-bound traffic routes through a virtual private gateway. A developer has recently deployed a new AWS Lambda function using the AWS Management Console. This function must securely access a database hosted in the private subnet of the on-premises data center. What is the most appropriate configuration to enable this access?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct approach is to <strong>configure the Lambda function to run inside the VPC with the appropriate security group</strong>. When a Lambda function is attached to a VPC, it can be assigned to specific subnets and security groups. This allows the function to access private resources, such as a database in the on-premises data center, via existing Direct Connect connections and routing through the virtual private gateway—assuming appropriate routes and firewall rules are already configured.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p>Setting up a VPN connection is unnecessary in this case because the company already has a working Direct Connect setup. Creating a redundant VPN tunnel introduces additional complexity and cost without adding value to this scenario.</p><p>Updating the VPC route tables does not directly allow a Lambda function to access the on-premises database unless the function is attached to the VPC. Without VPC attachment, Lambda runs in an isolated environment that has no access to VPC routing or private subnets.</p><p>Creating an Elastic IP and sending traffic from Lambda through it is not possible. Lambda functions cannot bind to Elastic IPs directly unless they’re behind a NAT Gateway, and even then, it would not allow secure, private access to an internal database in the on-premises network.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This scenario highlights real-world hybrid connectivity involving Lambda, Direct Connect, and VPC integration. Understanding how to enable private Lambda-to-on-premises communication is key in modern, serverless enterprise architectures.</p><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> When Lambda needs to access private or on-premises resources, always check whether it should be attached to a VPC. Once inside the VPC, standard routing and security group logic applies. Don't confuse internet routing (Elastic IPs, NAT) with private connectivity (Direct Connect or VPN).</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Configure the Lambda function to run in the VPC with the appropriate security group.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Set up a VPN connection from AWS to the data center. Route the traffic from the Lambda function through the VPN.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Update the route tables in the VPC to allow the Lambda function to access the on-premises data center through Direct Connect.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Elastic IP address. Configure the Lambda function to send traffic through the Elastic IP address without an elastic network interface.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 12,
  "query": "<p>A product engineering team uses a dedicated AWS account that is connected to the company's on-premises infrastructure via dual AWS Direct Connect links. All non-VPC-bound traffic is routed through a virtual private gateway. A new serverless component—a Lambda function—was created through the AWS Console and needs secure, low-latency access to a legacy database running in a private subnet within the company’s data center. What is the most appropriate way to ensure that the Lambda function can access the on-premises database?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct solution is to <strong>configure the Lambda function to run in the VPC with the appropriate security group</strong>. When a Lambda function is associated with a VPC, it can use private subnets that have routes to the virtual private gateway connected via AWS Direct Connect. The function is then able to securely communicate with resources in the on-premises network, including private databases, as long as security group and routing configurations are correct.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Setting up a VPN connection</strong> is unnecessary because Direct Connect is already in place and designed for high-throughput, low-latency, private connectivity between AWS and the on-premises data center. Adding a VPN would add cost and complexity without providing any additional benefit in this context.</p><p><strong>Updating the VPC route tables</strong> is insufficient if the Lambda function is not running inside the VPC. Lambda functions that are not VPC-connected operate in a separate managed environment and do not honor VPC route tables. Simply adding a route will not bridge the function to the data center unless the function is explicitly VPC-attached.</p><p><strong>Creating an Elastic IP address</strong> and trying to route Lambda traffic through it without an elastic network interface is technically invalid. Lambda functions do not support direct use of Elastic IPs and cannot send outbound traffic through them unless they are configured behind a NAT gateway or similar construct—none of which help in connecting to a private on-premises resource.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This scenario tests the candidate’s understanding of hybrid connectivity and serverless networking. It's a common situation in enterprise environments where legacy systems need to integrate with modern, cloud-native components like Lambda.</p><p><strong>Exam tip:</strong> When a Lambda function must access private resources—either within AWS or on-premises—it must be configured to run inside the VPC. Once inside, security groups and routing rules determine access. Direct Connect and virtual private gateways support private traffic, but only from within the VPC infrastructure.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Configure the Lambda function to run in the VPC with the appropriate security group.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Set up a VPN connection from AWS to the data center. Route the traffic from the Lambda function through the VPN.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
