{
  "id": 3,
  "query": "<p>A fast-growing startup is launching a cloud-native ecommerce platform on AWS. The architecture includes a scalable web front end behind a load balancer, a containerized backend service, and a relational database for storing customer transactions. The company wants a solution that is fault-tolerant, minimizes operational overhead, and does not require extensive manual maintenance. Which combination of actions should a solutions architect recommend?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answers are: <strong>Deploy the relational database using Amazon RDS in Multi-AZ mode</strong> and <strong>use Amazon ECS with the Fargate launch type to run the containerized application layer.</strong></p><p><strong>Amazon RDS in Multi-AZ mode</strong> provides automatic failover, high availability, and durability without manual intervention. When Multi-AZ is enabled, Amazon RDS synchronously replicates data to a standby instance in another Availability Zone, helping the application stay available in the event of a failure.</p><p><strong>Amazon ECS with the Fargate launch type</strong> is a serverless compute engine for containers. It eliminates the need to provision or manage EC2 instances. Fargate scales automatically and is ideal for applications that require high availability with minimal operational effort. It aligns well with the startup's goals of minimizing manual maintenance and scaling based on demand.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Creating RDS read replicas in another Availability Zone</strong> does not provide automatic failover. Read replicas are intended for read scalability, not high availability. Failover in this setup would require manual promotion and additional orchestration.</p><p><strong>Deploying a container cluster using Amazon ECS with EC2 launch type</strong> involves managing and scaling EC2 instances manually or with Auto Scaling groups. This adds operational overhead compared to using Fargate.</p><p><strong>Using Docker directly on EC2 instances</strong> lacks built-in orchestration, monitoring, and scaling capabilities. It places the burden of managing container scheduling, fault tolerance, and system patches on the operations team, which contradicts the objective of minimizing manual intervention.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question tests a candidate’s ability to design for high availability, understand container orchestration strategies on AWS, and distinguish between compute launch types. Many examinees confuse high availability (like Multi-AZ failover) with scalability (like read replicas).</p><p><strong>Tip for similar questions:</strong> Look for keywords like 'highly available', 'minimal manual intervention', and 'container-based'. These clues point toward managed services like Fargate and automatic failover solutions like RDS Multi-AZ deployments. Avoid answers that introduce EC2 management responsibilities unless the scenario specifically requires low-level control.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon RDS database with Multi-AZ deployment to ensure high availability and automatic failover.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create Amazon RDS read replicas in a separate Availability Zone to improve redundancy and availability.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Run Docker containers on Amazon EC2 instances and manually manage scaling and updates.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon ECS with the Fargate launch type to host the containerized application backend.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Deploy the containerized application on Amazon ECS using the EC2 launch type to maintain full infrastructure control.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
