{
  "id": 7,
  "query": "<p>An online travel booking platform uses Amazon RDS for MySQL to manage customer bookings and profile data. During peak hours, staff members frequently run one-off reporting queries that are causing slowdowns and timeouts in the main booking system. The business wants to prevent these timeouts while still allowing staff to access report data in real time. What is the best solution to meet these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Provision an Amazon RDS read replica and direct the reporting queries to the read replica.</strong></p><p>Amazon RDS read replicas are designed to offload read-heavy workloads from the primary database. By routing long-running or complex reporting queries to the read replica, you reduce contention on the primary database, ensuring that order processing and transactional performance remain unaffected. Read replicas can be created with minimal configuration and remain in sync with the primary instance through asynchronous replication.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Distributing both the reporting and transactional workloads across the primary and read replica</strong> defeats the purpose of workload isolation. Read replicas are not suitable for handling writes or transactional updates, and using them this way can lead to data inconsistencies and operational challenges.</p><p><strong>Migrating the application to Amazon DynamoDB</strong> is not a practical solution in this scenario. DynamoDB is a NoSQL database and would require a full application redesign and data model change, which is unnecessary just to solve read contention.</p><p><strong>Scheduling reports outside of business hours</strong> may temporarily mitigate the issue but doesn’t meet the real-time needs of staff who need access to reports during working hours. It also does not scale well and relies on manual or time-based restrictions.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question tests your understanding of how to scale relational databases and how to offload read-intensive operations using RDS features. It also reinforces the concept of workload separation and fault tolerance in high-availability environments.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When a question mentions read performance issues and reporting or analytical queries, look for options involving read replicas or caching layers. Be cautious of options that involve disruptive migrations or mixing read and write traffic on replicas.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Provision a read replica of the RDS database and redirect the reporting queries to the replica.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Set up a read replica and use both the primary and the replica instance to handle read and write operations.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Rebuild the ordering application to use Amazon DynamoDB with on-demand throughput for scalability.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Delay execution of staff reporting queries until after peak business hours to reduce load.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 8,
  "query": "<p>A healthcare institution is digitizing a vast archive of handwritten medical files and plans to continuously upload hundreds of new patient documents each day to the AWS Cloud. The organization’s data team is responsible for scanning and uploading these documents. A solutions architect must design a scalable, cost-efficient system that can automatically analyze the documents, extract clinical information, and make the extracted data available for SQL-based querying by a downstream application. What steps should the architect implement?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answers are: <strong>Store the extracted document data in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena for SQL-based analysis</strong> and <strong>Trigger an AWS Lambda function when new files are uploaded to use Amazon Textract and Amazon Comprehend Medical to extract structured medical insights.</strong></p><p><strong>Amazon S3 + Athena</strong> provides a highly scalable, serverless solution for storing and querying large datasets. When you store structured or semi-structured data in S3 and define an external table in Athena, you can run standard SQL queries without provisioning or managing infrastructure, which meets the requirement for operational efficiency and scalability.</p><p><strong>Amazon Textract</strong> automatically extracts text and structured data such as tables and forms from scanned documents. <strong>Amazon Comprehend Medical</strong> then performs natural language processing (NLP) to identify medical entities such as diagnoses, medications, and procedures. By combining these in a Lambda function triggered by new S3 uploads, the solution is fully serverless and scalable.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Writing the data to an Amazon EC2 instance with a MySQL database</strong> introduces unnecessary operational overhead, limits scalability, and contradicts the requirement for efficiency. EC2-based solutions require patching, scaling, and availability management.</p><p><strong>Using an Auto Scaling group of EC2 instances to process documents</strong> is overly complex compared to the serverless approach. It adds maintenance and cost while offering no major benefits over Lambda for this use case.</p><p><strong>Using Amazon Rekognition and Amazon Transcribe Medical</strong> is a misuse of the services. Rekognition is used for image and facial analysis, not text extraction. Transcribe Medical is for audio transcription, not scanned text documents.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question assesses your understanding of AWS serverless data pipelines, OCR with Textract, NLP with Comprehend Medical, and serverless querying using Athena. These are key tools for processing unstructured healthcare data at scale.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When a question involves scanned documents and structured data extraction, think of <em>Amazon Textract</em>. For medical insights, <em>Amazon Comprehend Medical</em> is the go-to NLP service. To support querying at scale, <em>Amazon S3</em> and <em>Athena</em> provide a powerful, low-maintenance combination.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Write the document information to an Amazon EC2 instance running a MySQL database for real-time storage and querying.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Store the document data in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena to perform SQL queries on the extracted information.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Set up an Auto Scaling group of EC2 instances to analyze and extract data from the scanned files using a custom script.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Trigger a Lambda function on file upload that uses Amazon Rekognition and Amazon Transcribe Medical to convert the text and extract insights.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use an AWS Lambda function triggered on file upload to extract text with Amazon Textract and analyze it with Amazon Comprehend Medical.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 9,
  "query": "<p>An analytics company runs a data aggregation workload using Amazon EC2 instances. The application performs frequent and repetitive read operations on multiple Amazon RDS databases as part of a nightly batch process. The database read activity is contributing to performance bottlenecks. A solutions architect needs to reduce the load on the RDS databases and improve efficiency while maintaining high availability. What is the best solution?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Use Amazon ElastiCache for Redis to cache frequently accessed data and reduce repeated reads from the RDS databases.</strong></p><p>Amazon ElastiCache for Redis is a highly available, in-memory data store that can cache frequent queries or computation results. This reduces the load on the RDS database by serving repetitive reads from the cache. Redis supports complex data types and offers features like replication, automatic failover, and persistence, making it ideal for high-performance and highly available caching scenarios.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Adding Amazon RDS read replicas</strong> can distribute read traffic but doesn’t eliminate redundant queries or optimize performance for repeatable lookups. Also, read replicas still introduce some latency and potential replication lag, and they do not provide the same in-memory speed benefits as a caching layer.</p><p><strong>Amazon Route 53 DNS caching</strong> is related to DNS resolution and routing, not application-level data caching or database optimization. It would have no effect on reducing RDS read traffic.</p><p><strong>Using Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached</strong> is a valid option for simple key-value caching, but Memcached lacks features such as persistence, advanced data structures, and built-in replication for high availability. Redis is the preferred choice when high availability and complex caching are needed.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question tests your ability to optimize data access patterns and reduce pressure on relational databases using appropriate AWS services. Choosing between caching and read replicas is a common design decision in performance tuning.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When you see repeated reads, latency issues, or unnecessary load on RDS, consider caching first. Use Redis for more advanced use cases requiring HA, replication, or data persistence. Memcached is suitable for simple, ephemeral, and non-critical caching.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Add Amazon RDS read replicas to offload and balance the database read workload.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Implement Amazon ElastiCache for Redis to cache frequently accessed data and reduce database load.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Configure Amazon Route 53 to cache DNS queries and reduce connection time to the database endpoints.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Deploy Amazon ElastiCache for Memcached to cache RDS queries with basic key-value storage.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 10,
  "query": "<p>A financial technology company is deploying a mission-critical application on AWS. The application's database must run on Amazon EC2 and must be highly available, with automatic failover in the event of an outage. The solution must ensure continuous operations with minimal manual intervention if a failure occurs. What is the most appropriate setup?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Deploy two EC2 instances in separate Availability Zones within the same Region, configure database clustering, and implement synchronous replication for automatic failover.</strong></p><p>This architecture provides high availability by using two EC2 instances located in different Availability Zones (AZs), reducing the risk of a single point of failure. By clustering the database and enabling replication, the system can automatically fail over to the secondary node if the primary instance becomes unavailable. This approach is commonly used in self-managed database high availability setups on EC2.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Launching a single EC2 instance with AMI backups and CloudFormation</strong> introduces manual intervention in the event of failure. While CloudFormation can help re-provision resources, this does not offer automatic failover and downtime is likely during recovery.</p><p><strong>Launching EC2 instances across different AWS Regions with replication</strong> improves disaster recovery (DR), but not high availability (HA). Cross-Region failover is typically slower and more complex to automate, and does not provide the immediate resilience required for HA.</p><p><strong>Using EC2 automatic recovery for a single instance</strong> is useful for recovering from hardware issues in the same AZ, but it does not address AZ-wide failures or provide true HA. Additionally, there's still potential downtime during the recovery period.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question evaluates your ability to architect resilient EC2-based database solutions. It reinforces the importance of Availability Zones and clustering when RDS is not used and a self-managed database on EC2 is required.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> For EC2-based HA architectures, always look for multi-AZ deployments with clustering or replication. If the solution mentions failover and high availability together, single-instance solutions or DR-only designs are not sufficient.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Launch two EC2 instances in different Availability Zones within the same Region, install the database, configure clustering, and enable replication for failover.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Launch one EC2 instance with the database and create regular AMI snapshots. Use AWS CloudFormation to re-create the instance after a failure.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Deploy two EC2 instances across separate AWS Regions with replication, and switch Regions manually if an outage occurs.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use a single EC2 instance with AMI backups and rely on EC2 automatic recovery to restart it if needed.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
