{
  "id": 13,
  "query": "<p>A software company has a Microsoft .NET-based application currently running on an on-premises Windows Server. The application relies on an Oracle Database Standard Edition for backend storage. The company wants to migrate this setup to AWS with minimal code changes. The target solution should ensure high availability while requiring as few development modifications as possible. What combination of actions should the company take to meet these goals?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answers are: <strong>Rehost the application using AWS Elastic Beanstalk with the .NET platform in a Multi-AZ deployment</strong> and <strong>Use AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) to migrate the Oracle database to Amazon RDS for Oracle with Multi-AZ support.</strong></p><p><strong>Elastic Beanstalk</strong> provides a managed platform for deploying applications, including .NET on Windows Server, with built-in support for high availability and Multi-AZ deployments. This allows the company to lift and shift the existing application with minimal code refactoring.</p><p><strong>AWS DMS</strong> enables seamless migration from an on-premises Oracle database to Amazon RDS for Oracle. RDS for Oracle supports Standard Edition, and Multi-AZ deployments provide automatic failover and high availability with minimal configuration. This preserves compatibility while reducing operational burden.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Refactoring the application to serverless using AWS Lambda</strong> and .NET Core would require significant code changes, especially since the current application is built for a traditional Windows Server environment. This contradicts the requirement to minimize development changes.</p><p><strong>Replatforming the application to Amazon EC2 with Amazon Linux AMI</strong> is not feasible without extensive redevelopment, as .NET applications typically depend on the Windows ecosystem.</p><p><strong>Migrating the Oracle database to DynamoDB</strong> would require a complete overhaul of the application’s data model and logic. DynamoDB is a NoSQL database and is not compatible with relational database features used by Oracle.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question evaluates the candidate’s understanding of AWS migration strategies—rehost, replatform, and refactor—and the ability to select services that meet both operational and development requirements while ensuring high availability.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When minimal code change is required, think of <em>rehosting</em> or <em>replatforming</em> to a managed environment. Combine this with a matching managed database engine (like RDS for Oracle) to preserve compatibility and reduce operational overhead.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Refactor the application into serverless architecture using AWS Lambda with .NET Core to modernize the workload.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
