{
  "id": 14,
  "query": "<p>An enterprise organization is operating a containerized microservices application on a self-managed Kubernetes cluster in its on-premises data center. The application relies on MongoDB for persistent data storage. The company is planning to move part of the workload to AWS but cannot modify application code or deployment strategies during the initial migration. The company also wants to reduce operational overhead as much as possible. What solution best satisfies these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Use Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (Amazon EKS) with AWS Fargate for compute and Amazon DocumentDB (with MongoDB compatibility) for data storage.</strong></p><p><strong>Amazon EKS</strong> provides a managed Kubernetes control plane that allows the organization to migrate its workloads without rewriting or replatforming the application. <strong>Fargate</strong> further reduces operational overhead by eliminating the need to provision and manage EC2 instances. <strong>Amazon DocumentDB</strong> offers MongoDB-compatible APIs, allowing the existing MongoDB-based application to work with minimal to no code changes while benefiting from a fully managed, scalable database service.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Amazon ECS with EC2 and MongoDB on EC2</strong> does not meet the constraint of keeping the deployment model the same. ECS is not Kubernetes, so this would require changes in how the application is deployed. Additionally, running MongoDB on EC2 means the company must manage the database infrastructure themselves, increasing operational burden.</p><p><strong>Amazon ECS with Fargate and DynamoDB</strong> violates two constraints: ECS is not compatible with Kubernetes, and DynamoDB is a NoSQL key-value store that is not MongoDB-compatible, which would require code changes.</p><p><strong>Amazon EKS with EC2 and DynamoDB</strong> maintains Kubernetes for compute but still introduces DynamoDB, which does not support MongoDB APIs. The application would need to be rewritten to support DynamoDB's data model and APIs.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question tests a candidate’s understanding of lift-and-shift migration strategies for containerized workloads and highlights the importance of choosing compatible and managed services when development flexibility is limited.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When migrating Kubernetes-based applications with MongoDB dependencies, prioritize services that offer compatibility and managed infrastructure. Amazon EKS supports Kubernetes as-is, and DocumentDB enables MongoDB workloads to move without major refactoring.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) with Amazon EC2 worker nodes for compute and MongoDB on EC2 for data storage.",
