{
  "id": 15,
  "query": "<p>A telemarketing firm is building a cloud-based contact center solution on AWS. The company requires the ability to identify and distinguish between multiple speakers during customer calls and generate transcripts of those conversations. These transcripts must be stored securely for at least 7 years for audit compliance. The business also wants to run queries on the transcript files to identify sales patterns and customer sentiment. What is the most effective solution to meet these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Use Amazon Transcribe for multiple speaker recognition and transcription. Store the transcript files in Amazon S3, and use Amazon Athena to analyze the files with SQL queries.</strong></p><p><strong>Amazon Transcribe</strong> provides real-time and batch transcription capabilities, including automatic speaker identification, which is ideal for call center environments with multiple participants. <strong>Amazon S3</strong> offers durable, cost-effective, and long-term storage with support for lifecycle policies to retain data for 7 years or longer. <strong>Amazon Athena</strong> enables serverless, ad hoc querying of structured data directly from S3 using standard SQL, which is perfect for business pattern analysis.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Amazon Rekognition</strong> is used for image and video analysis, including facial recognition, but it does not perform audio transcription or speaker recognition for voice data.</p><p><strong>Amazon Translate</strong> is for translating text between languages and does not perform transcription or speaker separation.</p><p><strong>Amazon Textract</strong> is used to extract text from scanned documents and PDFs, not for analyzing audio transcripts or performing speech-to-text.</p><p><strong>Amazon Redshift</strong> is a powerful data warehouse, but it is more suitable for large-scale structured datasets. For querying S3-based transcript files without ETL pipelines, Athena is the more efficient, serverless solution.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question evaluates your understanding of AWS services for audio transcription, long-term data storage, and data analytics. It tests your ability to match AWS AI/ML and storage services to compliance-driven and data-intensive use cases.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When dealing with voice data and call recordings, <em>Amazon Transcribe</em> is the go-to service. Combine it with <em>S3 for storage</em> and <em>Athena for querying</em> to enable powerful, serverless data processing and retention.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon Rekognition for multiple speaker recognition. Store the transcript files in Amazon S3. Use machine learning models for transcript file analysis.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon Transcribe for multiple speaker recognition. Use Amazon Athena for transcript file analysis.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon Translate for multiple speaker recognition. Store the transcript files in Amazon Redshift. Use SQL queries for transcript file analysis.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon Rekognition for multiple speaker recognition. Store the transcript files in Amazon S3. Use Amazon Textract for transcript file analysis.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
