{
  "id": 201,
  "query": "<p>A media company is building a mobile engagement platform that delivers SMS confirmation messages to users of its app. Users must be able to reply to these SMS messages, and the responses must be retained for one year to support analytics and trend forecasting. What is the best AWS solution to meet these functional and storage requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Build an Amazon Pinpoint journey and configure Amazon Pinpoint to forward events to an Amazon Kinesis data stream for downstream processing and long-term storage.</strong></p><p><strong>Amazon Pinpoint</strong> is a service for managing two-way SMS messaging at scale. It enables applications to send SMS messages and receive user replies using dedicated numbers. By connecting Pinpoint to <strong>Amazon Kinesis</strong>, responses and events can be streamed for real-time processing and later archived to services like Amazon S3, meeting the 1-year retention requirement.</p><p><strong>Important note:</strong> Amazon Pinpoint is being retired. New customers will no longer be able to sign up after <strong>May 20, 2025</strong>, and the service will be fully decommissioned on <strong>October 30, 2026</strong>. Existing customers can continue to use it until that time. AWS recommends customers migrate engagement features (such as journeys and analytics) to <strong>Amazon Connect’s proactive engagement solutions</strong> and event collection to <strong>Amazon Kinesis</strong>. The underlying messaging APIs (SMS, voice, push, WhatsApp, etc.) have been renamed as part of <strong>AWS End User Messaging</strong> and will remain supported beyond Pinpoint’s retirement.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Amazon Connect</strong> is primarily intended for voice and customer support contact center solutions. While it can be extended for outbound engagement, it does not natively support SMS response capture and real-time analytics in the same integrated manner as Pinpoint (or its successors).</p><p><strong>Amazon SQS</strong> is a message queuing service and is not designed for sending or receiving SMS messages. It lacks two-way communication capabilities for mobile messaging workflows.</p><p><strong>Amazon SNS FIFO topics</strong> are not used for SMS messaging or two-way communication. SNS supports basic outbound SMS but has no built-in mechanisms for tracking replies or integrating analytics pipelines.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question tests your understanding of AWS’s messaging and analytics services for mobile engagement workflows, and your ability to build solutions that support two-way SMS, long-term storage, and serverless analytics integration.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> For current use, <em>Amazon Pinpoint</em> combined with <em>Kinesis</em> is still a powerful solution, but plan for long-term migration to <em>Amazon Connect proactive engagement</em> and <em>AWS End User Messaging APIs</em> to remain compliant with AWS service lifecycle updates.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon Connect contact flow to send SMS messages and use a Lambda function to capture user responses.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
