{
  "id": 203,
  "query": "<p>A financial services company allows customers to schedule appointments with advisors by sending SMS messages. A web application running on Amazon EC2 processes incoming requests and publishes them to an Amazon SQS queue. A second EC2-based application pulls messages from the queue, sends meeting invites and confirmation emails, and stores finalized appointments in Amazon DynamoDB. As business grows, customers report increasing delays in receiving meeting invitations. What should a solutions architect recommend to address the performance issue?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Add an Auto Scaling group for the application that sends meeting invitations and configure it to scale based on the SQS queue depth.</strong></p><p>Scaling the consumer application based on the SQS queue depth ensures that more EC2 instances are added when the backlog of appointment requests grows. This improves processing throughput and reduces delays in sending confirmations and invitations. SQS queue metrics, such as <em>ApproximateNumberOfMessagesVisible</em>, can be used as a scaling trigger.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Adding a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)</strong> improves read latency for DynamoDB but does not help with the current bottleneck, which is the time taken to process and send meeting invitations—not database read performance.</p><p><strong>Introducing Amazon API Gateway</strong> in front of the web application may help with scaling the request ingestion layer, but the reported issue is not with request handling—it's with downstream message processing speed.</p><p><strong>Adding Amazon CloudFront</strong> improves the delivery of static content and cached responses but has no impact on backend processing of asynchronous appointment logic or SQS message handling.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question tests your understanding of decoupled architectures using SQS and how to scale consumer workloads based on queue depth, a common pattern for maintaining performance under load.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When faced with delays in asynchronous workflows that use SQS, always check if the consumer application can scale based on queue depth. Look at metrics like message age and backlog to identify performance bottlenecks.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Add a DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) cluster in front of the DynamoDB database.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Add an Amazon API Gateway API in front of the web application that accepts the appointment requests.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Add an Amazon CloudFront distribution. Set the origin as the web application that accepts the appointment requests.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Add an Auto Scaling group for the application that sends meeting invitations. Configure the Auto Scaling group to scale based on the depth of the SQS queue.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 204,
  "query": "<p>An e-commerce platform serves over 50 million active users and processes more than 25,000 transactions daily. The company stores customer purchase history in Amazon S3 and additional profile data in Amazon RDS. The analytics and product teams need secure, centralized access to all customer data for querying and reporting. The solution must support fine-grained access controls while minimizing operational overhead. What should a solutions architect recommend?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Create a data lake using AWS Lake Formation. Register the S3 bucket in Lake Formation, and create an AWS Glue connection to Amazon RDS. Use Lake Formation’s access control features to manage permissions across teams.</strong></p><p><strong>AWS Lake Formation</strong> provides centralized data cataloging and fine-grained, table-level, column-level, and row-level access control for data stored in S3 and other sources. By integrating Amazon RDS using an AWS Glue JDBC connection and registering the S3 data lake location, Lake Formation allows different teams to securely query a unified view of the data with minimal manual access management.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Migrating the purchase data to Amazon RDS</strong> centralizes the data but introduces high operational overhead, reduces scalability, and is not ideal for large-scale analytics workloads, especially for semi-structured or large-volume data.</p><p><strong>Scheduling a Lambda function to copy data from RDS to S3 and using Athena</strong> enables ad hoc querying but lacks native fine-grained access control. S3 bucket policies are coarse-grained and difficult to manage securely at scale across multiple teams.</p><p><strong>Creating an Amazon Redshift cluster and copying data</strong> provides powerful analytics but requires data replication and periodic ingestion pipelines. Redshift access controls are not as granular or centrally governed as those offered by Lake Formation, and this approach adds operational complexity.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question tests your understanding of modern data lake architectures and the role of Lake Formation in securing and governing access across diverse AWS data sources while minimizing manual overhead.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When a scenario mentions <em>data stored in both S3 and RDS</em>, requires <em>fine-grained access controls</em>, and wants <em>low operational overhead</em>, <strong>Lake Formation</strong> is usually the most secure and scalable answer.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Migrate the purchase data to write directly to Amazon RDS. Use RDS access controls to limit access.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Schedule an AWS Lambda function to periodically copy data from Amazon RDS to Amazon S3. Create an AWS Glue crawler. Use Amazon Athena to query the data. Use S3 policies to limit access.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a data lake by using AWS Lake Formation. Create an AWS Glue JDBC connection to Amazon RDS. Register the S3 bucket in Lake Formation. Use Lake Formation access controls to limit access.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon Redshift cluster. Schedule an AWS Lambda function to periodically copy data from Amazon S3 and Amazon RDS to Amazon Redshift. Use Amazon Redshift access controls to limit access.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 205,
  "query": "<p>A company hosts a marketing website in its on-premises data center. The site serves only static documents and is maintained by uploading files via an SFTP client. The company plans to migrate this site to AWS and deliver the content through Amazon CloudFront. The solution must be cost-effective, support SFTP uploads, and provide high availability. What is the best option for hosting the website content as the origin for the CloudFront distribution?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Create a private Amazon S3 bucket. Use a bucket policy to allow access from a CloudFront origin access identity (OAI). Upload content by using the AWS CLI.</strong></p><p>Amazon S3 is the most cost-effective and resilient choice for hosting static content. Configuring the bucket as private and allowing access only through CloudFront via an origin access identity (OAI) improves security. Though the administrator currently uses SFTP, transitioning to the <strong>AWS CLI</strong> or an S3-compatible client for uploads is straightforward and minimizes costs and operational overhead.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Amazon Lightsail with a virtual server</strong> introduces unnecessary compute resources and ongoing costs. While it supports SFTP uploads, it does not provide the same durability or scalability as Amazon S3 and does not align with a static site’s needs.</p><p><strong>EC2 with Auto Scaling and a load balancer</strong> is overly complex and expensive for serving static documents. This architecture is meant for dynamic applications and is not suitable for a low-traffic, static marketing website.</p><p><strong>Using a public S3 bucket with AWS Transfer for SFTP</strong> allows for SFTP uploads, but public S3 buckets are discouraged due to security risks. S3 static website hosting does not support HTTPS unless served through CloudFront, and using public buckets weakens access controls compared to private buckets with OAI.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This scenario tests the ability to choose a simple, secure, and cost-efficient hosting solution for static content behind CloudFront. It highlights the importance of private S3 bucket configuration and modern content delivery practices.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> For static websites on AWS with CloudFront, private S3 buckets with OAI are almost always the correct choice. Transitioning from SFTP to CLI uploads is common and lowers long-term operational burden.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create a virtual server by using Amazon Lightsail. Configure the web server in the Lightsail instance. Upload website content by using an SFTP client.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an AWS Auto Scaling group for Amazon EC2 instances. Use an Application Load Balancer. Upload website content by using an SFTP client.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a private Amazon S3 bucket. Use an S3 bucket policy to allow access from a CloudFront origin access identity (OAI). Upload website content by using the AWS CLI.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a public Amazon S3 bucket. Configure AWS Transfer for SFTP. Configure the S3 bucket for website hosting. Upload website content by using the SFTP client.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 206,
  "query": "<p>An enterprise IT team wants to monitor the use of Amazon Machine Images (AMIs) within its AWS environment. Specifically, the team needs to receive alerts whenever someone creates an AMI by calling the <code>CreateImage</code> API operation in any account within the organization. The solution must be reliable and require the least amount of ongoing management. What is the best approach to meet these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Create an Amazon EventBridge (formerly CloudWatch Events) rule that filters for the <code>CreateImage</code> API call. Configure an Amazon SNS topic as the target to send notifications.</strong></p><p><strong>Amazon EventBridge</strong> (formerly CloudWatch Events) can directly capture AWS API calls logged by CloudTrail in near real time. You can create a rule that matches the <code>CreateImage</code> API action and immediately trigger an alert through <strong>Amazon SNS</strong> when the event occurs. This solution is serverless, scalable, and requires minimal maintenance.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Using a Lambda function to poll CloudTrail logs</strong> increases operational overhead and introduces delays. You must manage invocation frequency and permissions, and polling is less efficient than event-driven detection.</p><p><strong>Configuring CloudTrail with SNS on S3 delivery, then using Athena queries</strong> introduces unnecessary complexity and latency. You must wait for CloudTrail to deliver logs, then schedule Athena queries or trigger them indirectly—this is not real-time and adds more moving parts.</p><p><strong>Sending CloudTrail logs to an SQS FIFO queue and using Lambda to parse events</strong> is more complex than necessary for this use case. This approach increases cost, complexity, and the number of components to monitor.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question tests your knowledge of event-driven monitoring using EventBridge and CloudTrail integration, which is a best practice for real-time security and operations monitoring in AWS.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When monitoring for specific API actions, <strong>EventBridge + CloudTrail + SNS</strong> is often the most efficient and scalable solution with the least operational burden.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create an AWS Lambda function to query AWS CloudTrail logs and to send an alert when a CreateImage API call is detected.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Configure AWS CloudTrail with an Amazon SNS notification that occurs when updated logs are sent to Amazon S3. Use Amazon Athena to create a new table and to query on CreateImage when an API call is detected.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon EventBridge (Amazon CloudWatch Events) rule for the CreateImage API call. Configure the target as an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic to send an alert when a CreateImage API call is detected.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Configure an Amazon SQS FIFO queue as a target for AWS CloudTrail logs. Create an AWS Lambda function to send an alert to an Amazon SNS topic when a CreateImage API call is detected.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 207,
  "query": "<p>A software company offers an asynchronous API that ingests incoming user requests. Depending on the type of request, the system routes the request to a corresponding microservice. The API is fronted by Amazon API Gateway, and an AWS Lambda function stores the requests in Amazon DynamoDB before dispatching them. Due to budget constraints, the company has provisioned limited DynamoDB throughput. As the number of users grows, the company begins to experience write availability issues and is losing some incoming requests. What is the most effective way to address this issue without affecting the current user experience?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Use Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) with a Lambda function to buffer writes to DynamoDB.</strong></p><p>Amazon SQS provides a highly durable and scalable message queue that decouples the incoming request flow from downstream systems. By writing user requests to SQS first, the system can absorb request spikes even if DynamoDB is throttled. An AWS Lambda function can process the SQS messages and write them to DynamoDB at a steady rate, preventing dropped requests and smoothing out throughput consumption. This allows the system to scale without affecting real-time user interactions.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><strong>Adding throttling to API Gateway</strong> would reduce the request rate and protect DynamoDB, but it directly impacts users by limiting the number of allowed requests, which results in visible API failures or rejections.</p><p><strong>Using DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX)</strong> improves read performance by caching, but it does not address write throughput or buffering. In this use case, the issue is related to writing to DynamoDB, not reading from it.</p><p><strong>Creating a secondary index</strong> only provides an alternative access pattern and does not improve write throughput or reduce the rate of request loss. In fact, adding indexes can slightly increase write costs and capacity consumption.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This question tests your knowledge of designing resilient, decoupled serverless architectures that can handle variable workloads. Understanding how to use SQS as a buffer for write-intensive operations is critical in distributed systems design on AWS.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When you're hitting throughput limits or experiencing throttling with DynamoDB, consider <em>SQS or Kinesis</em> to decouple producers from consumers. This provides elasticity and protects your data from being dropped during peak traffic periods.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Add throttling on the API Gateway with server-side throttling limits.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use DynamoDB Accelerator (DAX) and Lambda to buffer writes to DynamoDB.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a secondary index in DynamoDB for the table with the user requests.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use the Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) queue and Lambda to buffer writes to DynamoDB.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 208,
  "query": "<p>A company needs to securely transfer data from an Amazon EC2 instance to an Amazon S3 bucket. To meet internal security requirements, the solution must ensure that:</p><ul><li>No API calls or data are routed over the public internet.</li><li>Only the EC2 instance has permission to upload data to the S3 bucket.</li></ul><p>Which solution will meet these requirements with the least operational overhead?</p>",
  "answer": "<p><strong>Correct answer: Create a gateway VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 in the Availability Zone where the EC2 instance is located. Attach appropriate security groups to the endpoint. Attach a resource policy to the S3 bucket to only allow the EC2 instance’s IAM role for access.</strong></p><p>This solution ensures all traffic between the EC2 instance and the S3 bucket stays on the AWS private network, avoiding the public internet. A gateway VPC endpoint is the correct option for Amazon S3, and bucket policies can restrict access to only the specific IAM role associated with the EC2 instance.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Interface VPC endpoint:</strong> S3 uses gateway endpoints, not interface endpoints. Interface endpoints are used for services like SSM, Secrets Manager, etc.</li><li><strong>nslookup to get S3 IP:</strong> Amazon S3 is a distributed system and does not provide a static IP that can be routed to in this way. This approach is unreliable and not recommended.</li><li><strong>Using ip-ranges.json:</strong> IP ranges may change and do not provide control over routing paths. There is no guarantee that the data avoids the public internet using this method.</li></ul><p><strong>Key takeaway:</strong> For private and secure access to Amazon S3 from within a VPC, use a <em>gateway VPC endpoint</em> and restrict bucket access using IAM policies and bucket resource policies.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create an interface VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 in the subnet where the EC2 instance is located. Attach a resource policy to the S3 bucket to only allow the EC2 instance’s IAM role for access.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a gateway VPC endpoint for Amazon S3 in the Availability Zone where the EC2 instance is located. Attach appropriate security groups to the endpoint. Attach a resource policy to the S3 bucket to only allow the EC2 instance’s IAM role for access.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Run the nslookup tool from inside the EC2 instance to obtain the private IP address of the S3 bucket’s service API endpoint. Create a route in the VPC route table to provide the EC2 instance with access to the S3 bucket. Attach a resource policy to the S3 bucket to only allow the EC2 instance’s IAM role for access.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use the AWS provided, publicly available ip-ranges.json file to obtain the private IP address of the S3 bucket’s service API endpoint. Create a route in the VPC route table to provide the EC2 instance with access to the S3 bucket. Attach a resource policy to the S3 bucket to only allow the EC2 instance’s IAM role for access.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
