{
  "id": 210,
  "query": "<p>A company is designing a cloud-native application to run on Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances across multiple Availability Zones. The EC2 instances are part of an Auto Scaling group that adjusts capacity throughout the day. An Application Load Balancer (ALB) distributes incoming traffic evenly. The application requires a scalable and resilient solution for storing user session state as instances frequently scale in and out. The development team is open to modifying the application to support this need.</p><p>What is the MOST suitable approach to implement shared session management in this architecture?</p>",
  "answer": "<p><strong>Correct answer: Integrate Amazon ElastiCache to persist session data in memory.</strong></p><p>Amazon ElastiCache (using Redis or Memcached) is a fully managed, low-latency, in-memory data store ideal for storing transient session data. It allows any EC2 instance to access a common session state, which is critical in a dynamic auto scaling environment with distributed workloads. This architecture supports high availability and resilience across Availability Zones.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Enable sticky sessions on the ALB to maintain session affinity:</strong> Sticky sessions tie a user to a specific instance, which undermines fault tolerance and breaks when instances are terminated or replaced during scaling events.</li><li><strong>Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager to track user sessions:</strong> This tool is meant for secure administrative shell access to EC2, not for handling web application session data.</li><li><strong>Generate temporary credentials with AWS STS for each user session:</strong> AWS STS issues temporary credentials for authentication, not for managing application session data.</li></ul><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> Amazon ElastiCache enables scalable and centralized session storage that aligns well with dynamic EC2-based architectures.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Integrate Amazon ElastiCache to persist session data in memory.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Enable sticky sessions on the ALB to maintain session affinity.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager to track user sessions.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Generate temporary credentials with AWS STS for each user session.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 200,
  "query": "<p>An education technology startup is launching a web-based learning platform on AWS. The application backend will run on Amazon EC2 On-Demand Instances spread across several Availability Zones to support elasticity and high availability. These EC2 instances will frequently scale in and out depending on usage spikes during class times. An Application Load Balancer (ALB) is used to distribute incoming traffic evenly. The architecture must support centralized session state management so that users can seamlessly continue their sessions even when traffic is redirected to a different instance. The development team is open to implementing changes in the application code if necessary.</p><p>Which approach best supports this requirement?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Leverage Amazon ElastiCache to store and manage session data externally.</strong></p><p>This approach decouples session state from individual EC2 instances and centralizes it in a fast, managed in-memory data store like Redis or Memcached. This allows any EC2 instance behind the load balancer to access session information regardless of where the user was initially connected. This architecture supports horizontal scaling and is ideal for distributed environments where sessions must persist across instance changes.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Enable sticky sessions (session affinity) on the Application Load Balancer</em> is not ideal for dynamic scaling environments. Sticky sessions bind a user session to a specific instance, but when instances scale in or out, the session may be lost or unavailable. This method also introduces uneven load distribution over time.</p><p><em>Use Session Manager from AWS Systems Manager to handle session tracking</em> is unrelated to application-level session management. Session Manager is designed for remote shell access and auditing, not for managing user sessions in web applications.</p><p><em>Use AWS STS's GetSessionToken API to manage session lifecycle</em> is focused on temporary credentials and identity federation, not user session state in web applications. It’s not suitable for application-level session tracking.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It tests your understanding of state management in horizontally scaled architectures. Many AWS exam questions require knowing when to externalize state to achieve elasticity. Recognizing when a service like ElastiCache fits into distributed systems is key.</p><p><strong>Hint for solving this type of question:</strong> If an application is scaling across instances or zones, assume local state cannot be relied on. Look for options that move state to shared, external services.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Leverage Amazon ElastiCache to store and manage session data externally.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Configure sticky session support on the ALB to retain user connections to specific EC2 instances.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Implement AWS Systems Manager Session Manager to retain user session context across connections.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use AWS STS GetSessionToken to issue session-based tokens for application tracking.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 211,
  "query": "<p>A national grocery chain has launched an online grocery pickup platform that is growing in popularity. To handle increasing customer demand, the platform uses two separate fleets of Amazon EC2 instances managed by Auto Scaling groups. The first fleet handles incoming customer orders, and the second is responsible for validating and dispatching those orders to local stores. While capturing the orders is fast, the validation and dispatch process often takes longer due to inventory checks and coordination with store locations. No customer order data can be lost, even during scale-in or scale-out events. The system must be optimized for cost and performance during high-demand periods.</p><p>What architectural change will best meet these needs?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Set up two Amazon SQS queues—one for receiving orders and another for dispatching them. Have the EC2 instances poll the queues and adjust Auto Scaling based on backlog-per-instance metrics.</strong></p><p>This architecture decouples the order intake and fulfillment processes using durable queues that act as buffers, ensuring no order is lost during traffic spikes or scaling actions. By defining a backlog-per-instance metric, the Auto Scaling groups can scale dynamically based on the actual workload, leading to efficient use of compute resources and better system resilience.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Using CloudWatch metrics to preconfigure minimum instance counts based on peak usage</em> leads to overprovisioning and is not cost-effective. It also does not address the need for buffering or managing variable processing times.</p><p><em>Triggering CloudWatch alarms to launch new Auto Scaling groups via SNS</em> adds unnecessary complexity and is not a scalable or elegant way to manage instance elasticity. It does not inherently support decoupling or data durability.</p><p><em>Using SQS queues and scaling based on SQS-sent notifications</em> is a partial solution. While it decouples the processes, scaling based on generic notifications does not provide fine-grained control. Backlog-per-instance metrics are much more effective at representing real workload pressure.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It tests your knowledge of using messaging systems like SQS to decouple application components and how to scale infrastructure based on intelligent metrics instead of simple hardware indicators like CPU. AWS architects must know how to design resilient and cost-effective systems under dynamic loads.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When different components have uneven workloads or processing durations, use queues to buffer and allow each tier to scale independently. Look for scaling strategies that reflect business metrics (like queue length) rather than basic instance metrics.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Set up two Amazon SQS queues—one for receiving orders and another for dispatching them. Have the EC2 instances poll the queues and adjust Auto Scaling based on backlog-per-instance metrics.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Monitor CPU usage of the EC2 instances and define static minimum capacity values in each Auto Scaling group based on peak traffic predictions.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use CloudWatch alarms on CPU metrics to trigger an Amazon SNS topic that provisions additional Auto Scaling groups as traffic increases.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create two Amazon SQS queues and configure the EC2 instances to read from them. Let the queues send scaling notifications to Auto Scaling groups as needed.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
