{
  "id": 212,
  "query": "<p>A global media streaming company runs several critical applications in production. One of its microservice-based systems spans multiple AWS services including Amazon EC2, AWS Lambda, Amazon RDS, Amazon SQS, and Amazon SNS. These services are deployed across multiple AWS Regions to ensure low-latency performance. To manage resources efficiently, each component is tagged with a key named <code>application</code> and a value unique to the app it supports. A solutions architect needs to quickly produce a consolidated view of all the resources tagged for this application across all Regions.</p><p>Which approach provides the fastest and simplest way to retrieve this information?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Use the AWS Resource Groups Tag Editor to run a global query for resources tagged with the application key.</strong></p><p>The AWS Resource Groups Tag Editor is specifically designed to search, filter, and report on AWS resources based on their tags across multiple services and Regions. It provides a fast, centralized interface that avoids the need to manually query each Region or service. This is the most efficient way to retrieve a list of all components tagged for a specific application.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Using AWS CloudTrail to find resources with a specific tag</em> is not practical. CloudTrail logs API activity but does not provide a direct way to list resources by tag. It also involves parsing logs and is not optimized for this use case.</p><p><em>Querying each service and Region with the AWS CLI</em> is technically possible but extremely inefficient and slow. It would require writing custom scripts and manually aggregating results, which contradicts the requirement for a quick solution.</p><p><em>Running a query in Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights</em> only works if the services are logging relevant tag metadata into CloudWatch Logs, which is not guaranteed. It’s not designed to inventory tagged resources.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> This question tests your ability to efficiently retrieve resource metadata in a multi-Region, multi-service environment. Being familiar with AWS-native tools like the Tag Editor is crucial for visibility, compliance, and cost optimization tasks.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When asked to identify or report on resources based on tags across services or Regions, always consider Resource Groups Tag Editor first. It's a centralized and purpose-built tool for this exact scenario.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use the AWS Resource Groups Tag Editor to run a global query for resources tagged with the application key.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Query each AWS service individually using the AWS CLI across all Regions and compile the tagged resources manually.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Run a CloudWatch Logs Insights query to search for logs referencing application tags and infer the resources.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
