{
  "id": 212,
  "query": "<p>An analytics firm generates a daily export of its internal reporting database and stores each export in Amazon S3. The exported file sizes range from 3.5 GB to 6 GB and are used by various departments for unpredictable analytics workloads. Sometimes the data is accessed heavily right after upload; other times, it sits idle for days. However, all data must be accessible immediately upon request and retained for up to 4 months. The company wants the most cost-effective storage solution that maintains instant access at all times.</p><p>Which S3 storage class best meets these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>S3 Intelligent-Tiering</strong></p><p>S3 Intelligent-Tiering is designed for situations where access patterns are unpredictable. It automatically moves objects between frequent and infrequent access tiers based on usage, without impacting retrieval time. It also retains the same performance and latency characteristics as the S3 Standard class, making it ideal when immediate access is required but costs must be optimized over time. It’s particularly effective for data that may be accessed multiple times or not at all during its lifetime.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval</em> is optimized for archival data that is rarely accessed, although retrieval is faster than traditional Glacier. However, its use incurs additional costs if data is accessed frequently, which contradicts the 'variable and unpredictable' usage pattern mentioned in the scenario.</p><p><em>S3 Standard</em> offers excellent performance and immediate access, but it is not the most cost-efficient choice for data with changing access patterns over time. It charges the highest base storage rate and lacks built-in optimization for rarely accessed data.</p><p><em>S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA)</em> is cheaper than S3 Standard for long-lived, infrequently accessed data. However, it charges a retrieval fee and has a minimum storage duration charge of 30 days. It’s not ideal when access frequency is unpredictable and retrievals might be frequent in short bursts.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> This question assesses your knowledge of the trade-offs between AWS S3 storage classes and how to match usage patterns to the correct cost model. It emphasizes understanding data lifecycle and retrieval behaviors in selecting the most appropriate storage tier.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> If a scenario mentions unpredictable access patterns but requires immediate access and cost efficiency, S3 Intelligent-Tiering is often the best choice. Avoid using Glacier or IA classes unless the data is rarely accessed and retrieval costs or delays are acceptable.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "S3 Intelligent-Tiering",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "S3 Glacier Instant Retrieval",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "S3 Standard",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA)",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
}
