{
  "id": 213,
  "query": "<p>A startup is preparing to launch a new mobile application and is building its backend using AWS. To distribute incoming traffic, the team has implemented an Application Load Balancer (ALB). They need to defend the application from common web-based threats, such as SQL injection and cross-site scripting (XSS), while keeping infrastructure maintenance to a minimum. With a small DevOps team and limited time to manage servers or third-party software, they prefer a managed security solution that reduces their operational burden.</p><p>Which solution should the solutions architect recommend to meet these needs?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Implement AWS WAF and associate it with the Application Load Balancer.</strong></p><p>AWS WAF (Web Application Firewall) is a fully managed service that integrates directly with ALBs. It provides preconfigured rule groups and customizable filtering to defend against application-layer threats such as SQL injection and XSS. Because it is managed by AWS, it significantly reduces the operational responsibilities around patching, updating, and maintaining infrastructure. This makes it the best fit for organizations with limited security or operations teams.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Hosting the application using Amazon S3 with public access enabled</em> is only suitable for serving static websites, not dynamic backends typically served behind ALBs. This approach does not address traffic filtering or security for backend logic, and it exposes the content to the public without security controls.</p><p><em>Enabling AWS Shield Advanced and adding the ALB as a protected resource</em> helps mitigate DDoS attacks but does not specifically address application-layer threats like XSS or SQL injection. It's also a premium service with more complex configuration and cost, which may not suit smaller teams focused on application-level protection.</p><p><em>Routing traffic through a third-party firewall on an EC2 instance before reaching the ALB</em> increases the operational burden—contradicting the goal of reducing server maintenance. This option introduces more infrastructure to manage and maintain, which is not desirable for a small team.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It highlights the importance of choosing the right managed service based on the type of attack and the team's ability to manage infrastructure. Understanding the difference between network-layer (e.g., DDoS) and application-layer (e.g., XSS, SQLi) threats is essential when selecting security solutions on AWS.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> If the question focuses on preventing application-layer attacks with minimal overhead, AWS WAF is almost always the most appropriate answer—especially when integrated directly with an ALB.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Implement AWS WAF and attach it to the Application Load Balancer to block web-based threats.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
