{
  "id": 214,
  "query": "<p>A financial services company receives a continuous stream of raw transaction data in the form of CSV files, which are automatically uploaded throughout the day to a designated Amazon S3 bucket. These files must be converted into the Apache Parquet format and stored in a different S3 bucket optimized for analytical processing. The company wants to implement a low-maintenance solution that requires minimal coding and is easy to set up.</p><p>Which approach offers the simplest and most efficient solution to meet these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Set up an AWS Glue crawler to catalog the data and configure an AWS Glue ETL job to convert the CSV files to Parquet, writing the output to the target S3 bucket.</strong></p><p>AWS Glue is a serverless data integration service that requires minimal development effort. You can use a Glue crawler to automatically detect the schema of incoming CSV files, and an ETL job can be visually designed or written with minimal code to convert the files into Parquet format. Glue handles job orchestration, parallel processing, and integration with S3, making it ideal for this use case.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Creating an Amazon EMR cluster and writing a Spark application</em> provides fine-grained control and performance, but it involves substantial setup and maintenance overhead. It is not the most development-efficient option for this relatively straightforward transformation task.</p><p><em>Using AWS Batch with a Bash script to transform the data</em> requires writing and maintaining custom scripts, managing job definitions, and orchestrating the process manually. This adds complexity and operational burden, which contradicts the requirement for minimal development effort.</p><p><em>Developing an AWS Lambda function to process and convert the files</em> is feasible for small datasets, but Lambda has runtime limitations and memory constraints that can be problematic with large or frequent file transformations. It also requires more custom development compared to Glue’s native ETL capabilities.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It evaluates your understanding of AWS’s managed data processing services and your ability to choose solutions based on development effort and operational simplicity—important considerations in real-world, high-volume data pipelines.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When the goal is to transform structured data at scale with minimal coding or server management, AWS Glue is often the best-fit solution, especially when converting between formats like CSV and Parquet.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Set up an AWS Glue crawler and create an ETL job to convert the CSV files to Parquet format. Output the results to the designated S3 bucket.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Deploy an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Spark and write a Spark job to convert CSV to Parquet using EMRFS to write the output.",
      "correct": false,
