  "id": 218,
  "query": "<p>An online retail company hosts its website on an Amazon EC2 instance in a public subnet. The instance is assigned an Elastic IP address and currently uses the default security group. However, the default network ACL has been manually updated to deny all traffic in both directions. The company now needs to allow global HTTPS access to the website on port 443.</p><p>What combination of actions should a solutions architect take to enable secure web access to the EC2 instance from anywhere? (Select two.)</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct combination of actions is:</p><p><strong>1. Create a security group rule to allow inbound TCP traffic on port 443 from 0.0.0.0/0.<br>2. Update the network ACL to allow inbound TCP port 443 from 0.0.0.0/0 and outbound TCP ports 32768–65535 to 0.0.0.0/0.</strong></p><p>The security group acts as a virtual firewall for the EC2 instance. By default, security groups are stateful, so only an inbound rule is needed for HTTPS access (port 443). Allowing traffic from 0.0.0.0/0 means anyone can reach the server on that port.</p><p>Network ACLs are stateless, which means both inbound and outbound rules must be explicitly defined. To support a full TCP connection (like HTTPS), the inbound rule must allow traffic on port 443, and the outbound rule must allow ephemeral ports (typically TCP ports 32768–65535) for the return traffic. This ensures that responses from the EC2 instance are not blocked.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Allowing TCP port 443 to destination 0.0.0.0/0 in a security group</em> is not valid—security group rules do not use 'destination' for inbound rules. This syntax is incorrect and not applicable to AWS security groups.</p><p><em>Updating the network ACL to allow all traffic on port 443 in both directions</em> (with the same source and destination) is overly permissive and doesn't follow best practices. It also misses the need for ephemeral port ranges for return traffic in the outbound direction.</p><p><em>Creating a security group rule without updating the network ACL</em> would not help if the network ACL is still blocking all traffic. Both layers of access control must be correctly configured.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> This tests your understanding of the difference between security groups and network ACLs, as well as how AWS handles stateful and stateless access controls. It’s important to know how to configure both to ensure full, bidirectional connectivity for a public-facing EC2 instance.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Security groups are stateful—network ACLs are not. Always allow both inbound and outbound ports in ACLs for the traffic you want to support.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create a security group with a rule to allow TCP port 443 from source 0.0.0.0/0.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a security group with a rule to allow TCP port 443 to destination 0.0.0.0/0.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Update the network ACL to allow TCP port 443 from source 0.0.0.0/0.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Update the network ACL to allow inbound/outbound TCP port 443 from source 0.0.0.0/0 and to destination 0.0.0.0/0.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Update the network ACL to allow inbound TCP port 443 from source 0.0.0.0/0 and outbound TCP port 32768-65535 to destination 0.0.0.0/0.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 219,
  "query": "<p>A logistics company is experiencing performance issues with its core application hosted on Amazon EC2 instances. The application is stateful and performs memory-intensive, in-memory processing tasks. The infrastructure was provisioned using AWS CloudFormation and is currently using M5 instance types. As user traffic has grown, the system has become sluggish, and end users are now reporting delays when interacting with the application.</p><p>What is the most operationally efficient solution to address these performance issues?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Update the CloudFormation templates to switch from M5 to R5 EC2 instances. Deploy the Amazon CloudWatch agent on the instances to collect custom latency metrics for future tuning and planning.</strong></p><p>R5 instances are memory-optimized and well-suited for workloads that require high-performance in-memory processing. Since the application is stateful and memory-bound, increasing memory availability directly addresses the bottleneck. Using CloudFormation to implement the change ensures consistency and automation. Deploying the CloudWatch agent enables the collection of fine-grained, custom metrics (such as application latency), which supports better long-term capacity planning and performance optimization.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Switching to T3 instances and using the AWS Management Console</em> is not a good choice for memory-bound, high-performance applications. T3 is a burstable instance type meant for lightweight workloads and is unlikely to improve performance in a sustained high-demand scenario. Additionally, using the console lacks repeatability and control compared to CloudFormation.</p><p><em>Adding EC2 instances to an Auto Scaling group and manually adjusting capacity</em> helps with scaling, but does not address the fundamental problem of memory constraints. Also, manual intervention is less operationally efficient than an automated, metric-informed solution.</p><p><em>Switching to R5 instances and relying only on built-in EC2 memory metrics</em> may help, but Amazon EC2 does not natively expose memory utilization metrics. Without installing the CloudWatch agent, memory metrics and application-specific performance indicators (like latency) will be missing, limiting visibility and hindering optimization.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> This scenario tests your ability to recognize when instance type changes, rather than horizontal scaling, are the correct solution. It also emphasizes the importance of automated infrastructure updates using CloudFormation and proper monitoring practices with CloudWatch.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When applications are stateful and memory-bound, think in terms of vertical scaling and memory-optimized instances. For observability, always consider using the CloudWatch agent for custom metrics like memory and latency.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Update the CloudFormation templates to switch to R5 EC2 instances. Install the CloudWatch agent to capture application latency metrics for performance tuning.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Replace the M5 EC2 instances with T3 instances using the AWS Management Console and enable Auto Scaling.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Modify the CloudFormation templates to place EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group and manually scale the group as needed.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Change the instance type to R5 in the CloudFormation templates and rely on built-in EC2 memory metrics for future scaling decisions.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
