{
  "id": 220,
  "query": "<p>An e-learning company is designing a lightweight API using Amazon API Gateway to handle user-generated requests. The request traffic is highly unpredictable—hours may pass without activity, but during busy periods, dozens of requests might arrive in quick succession. Each request initiates a background task that must complete within a few seconds. The solution should minimize costs and infrastructure overhead while meeting these performance requirements.</p><p>Which AWS compute service should the solutions architect integrate with API Gateway to meet these needs most efficiently?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Use an AWS Lambda function.</strong></p><p>AWS Lambda is a serverless, event-driven compute service ideal for workloads with irregular traffic patterns. It automatically scales with demand, charges only for the time your code runs, and integrates natively with API Gateway. Since the background tasks complete within a few seconds and requests are sporadic, Lambda provides a highly cost-effective and operationally efficient solution.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Using an AWS Glue job</em> is inappropriate for this use case. AWS Glue is primarily used for ETL workloads and data transformation tasks that operate on structured data sources, not for near-real-time API-backed compute processing. It also has longer startup times and is not designed for latency-sensitive interactions.</p><p><em>Deploying a containerized service on Amazon EKS</em> is overkill for this scenario. EKS requires managing a Kubernetes control plane and worker nodes, leading to unnecessary complexity and costs, especially for an infrequent workload.</p><p><em>Hosting containers on Amazon ECS with EC2</em> also introduces fixed infrastructure costs since EC2 instances run continuously unless manually scaled down. This would result in idle resource charges during periods of no traffic, making it less cost-effective compared to a fully serverless model.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It evaluates your understanding of how to design cost-efficient, event-driven architectures using AWS serverless services like Lambda and API Gateway. It also helps assess your ability to match unpredictable workloads with scalable compute options.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> For unpredictable workloads where compute is needed only on demand and with short execution time, AWS Lambda is typically the optimal solution—especially when integrated with API Gateway.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use an AWS Lambda function.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Trigger an AWS Glue job to process the data asynchronously.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Invoke a containerized service running in Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service (EKS).",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Call a container-based API hosted on Amazon ECS using Amazon EC2 instances.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 221,
  "query": "<p>A media analytics company operates a Linux-based application on a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances. The application generates a high volume of log data daily. Due to regulatory compliance, the company is required to retain these logs for 7 years. A reporting system must be able to access the entire log history concurrently for analytics and auditing purposes. The solution must be as cost-effective as possible while meeting the access and retention requirements.</p><p>Which AWS storage option should the company choose?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Use Amazon S3.</strong></p><p>Amazon S3 is highly durable, scalable, and cost-effective for storing long-term data. It supports concurrent access to objects by multiple systems, making it ideal for analytics workloads. With features like S3 Lifecycle policies, the company can move older logs to more cost-efficient storage classes (e.g., S3 Glacier or Glacier Deep Archive) while keeping newer logs readily accessible. This allows them to meet both the 7-year retention requirement and the concurrent access needs at the lowest cost.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Amazon EBS</em> is block storage meant for individual EC2 instances. It does not natively support access from multiple instances and becomes expensive at scale for long-term storage.</p><p><em>Amazon EFS</em> is a shared file system for Linux workloads and does support concurrent access, but it is significantly more expensive than S3—especially for infrequently accessed archival data. It is better suited for active, low-latency file access.</p><p><em>Amazon EC2 instance store</em> is ephemeral. Data is lost when the instance is stopped or terminated, which makes it unsuitable for durable, long-term storage or compliance use cases.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It tests your knowledge of AWS storage types and how to select the most appropriate and economical option based on durability, access patterns, and retention requirements.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When you see a long-term retention requirement combined with concurrent access needs, think of Amazon S3 with lifecycle policies. It's often the best fit for compliance and analytical workloads.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon S3 to store the logs and apply lifecycle policies for cost-effective long-term retention.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Store logs on Amazon EBS volumes attached to each EC2 instance.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon EFS to store log files and share access across EC2 instances.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Write logs to the local instance store on each EC2 host.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 222,
  "query": "<p>An enterprise has contracted a third-party service provider to carry out tasks within the company’s AWS environment. The vendor operates an automated system hosted in their own AWS account, and they do not have direct IAM user access to the enterprise’s AWS account. The company wants to securely grant this tool permission to access resources in their AWS account without creating IAM users for the vendor.</p><p>What is the most secure and appropriate way for the company to provide access to the vendor's automated tool?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Create an IAM role in the company's account that trusts the vendor’s AWS account. Attach the required IAM policies to the role to define the vendor’s permissions.</strong></p><p>This solution uses cross-account IAM roles, a best practice for granting access between AWS accounts. The vendor’s tool can assume the role from their own account if the trust policy permits it. This avoids the need for IAM users, credentials, or manual access, and gives the company full control over what permissions are granted and for how long. It also enables auditing and logging via AWS CloudTrail.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Creating an IAM user in the company’s account</em> introduces credential management issues and violates least privilege principles. It also makes tracking access more difficult and is not scalable or secure for automated tools across accounts.</p><p><em>Creating an IAM group and attempting to add a user from another AWS account</em> is not technically possible. IAM groups cannot include users from external AWS accounts.</p><p><em>Creating an identity provider with type 'AWS account'</em> is a misunderstanding of identity federation. That option is used for integrating identity providers like SAML or OIDC-based systems, not for cross-account AWS-to-AWS access.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It tests your knowledge of secure cross-account access in AWS, especially in B2B contexts where one organization needs to give scoped, auditable access to another’s AWS resources.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When you need to allow services in another AWS account to access your resources, consider using IAM roles with trust policies targeting that account. Avoid IAM users in shared accounts.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create an IAM role in the company’s account that trusts the vendor’s AWS account and assign the required permissions via attached policies.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an IAM user in the company’s account with a strong password and assign policies for the vendor’s required access.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an IAM group in the company’s account and try to add the vendor’s IAM user from their account to this group.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Set up an identity provider in the IAM console using 'AWS account' as the provider type and configure it with the vendor's AWS account ID.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 223,
  "query": "<p>A software company is running a Java Spring Boot application inside an Amazon EKS pod within private subnets of a VPC. The application needs to write records to an Amazon DynamoDB table. For security reasons, the traffic must not traverse the public internet. A solutions architect is tasked with ensuring that the application can securely connect to DynamoDB without violating network or identity best practices.</p><p>Which combination of steps should the architect implement to meet these requirements? (Select two.)</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct combination is:<br><strong>1. Attach an IAM role with the required permissions to the EKS pod using IAM roles for service accounts (IRSA).<br>2. Create a VPC endpoint for DynamoDB.</strong></p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong><br>To avoid routing traffic over the internet, a VPC endpoint for DynamoDB must be created. This ensures all requests to DynamoDB are handled through the AWS private network within the VPC. To securely grant the pod access to DynamoDB, IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA) should be used, allowing the pod to assume a specific IAM role without embedding credentials.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Attaching an IAM user to the pod</em> is not a supported practice in AWS. IAM users are intended for individual human users, not applications or services, and credentials would have to be manually managed.</p><p><em>Allowing outbound traffic through the subnet’s network ACLs</em> is insufficient on its own. While necessary, it does not ensure that traffic stays private—without a VPC endpoint, traffic could still reach DynamoDB via the internet if a NAT gateway or internet gateway is in place.</p><p><em>Embedding access keys in the Java code</em> is a security anti-pattern. It creates significant risk of credential exposure and violates AWS best practices for secure application design.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It evaluates the candidate’s understanding of secure communication within a VPC, IAM best practices, and integrating AWS managed services with Kubernetes. These are key areas in modern cloud-native design.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When working with EKS and AWS services like DynamoDB, prefer IRSA over hardcoded credentials, and always use VPC endpoints to ensure private connectivity.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Attach an IAM role with the required permissions to the EKS pod using IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA).",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Attach an IAM user that has the necessary permissions directly to the EKS pod.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Allow outbound access through the private subnet’s network ACLs to enable connectivity.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Set up a VPC endpoint for DynamoDB to route traffic privately.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Include the IAM access keys in the application’s source code for authentication.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 224,
  "query": "<p>A media company recently rehosted its monolithic web application on Amazon EC2 instances within a single AWS Region. The company now wants to improve the application architecture to ensure high availability and fault tolerance. In addition, user requests must be evenly distributed across all healthy EC2 instances. The architecture should prevent a single point of failure and maintain random traffic distribution.</p><p>Which combination of actions should the company take to meet these goals? (Select two.)</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answers are:<br><strong>1. Launch four EC2 instances: two in one Availability Zone and two in another.<br>2. Create an Amazon Route 53 multivalue answer routing policy.</strong></p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong><br>To achieve high availability, EC2 instances should be distributed across multiple Availability Zones (AZs) within the same Region. This setup ensures that if one AZ goes down, the other can still serve traffic. Launching instances in multiple AZs helps build fault tolerance.</p><p>Using a <em>Route 53 multivalue answer routing policy</em> allows Route 53 to return multiple healthy IP addresses in response to DNS queries. This distributes traffic randomly across all healthy EC2 instances and supports health checks to ensure traffic is only routed to available targets.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Route 53 failover routing policy</em> is intended for active-passive architectures where traffic is routed to a primary resource unless it fails, at which point it fails over to a secondary. This doesn’t meet the requirement of distributing traffic randomly across all instances.</p><p><em>Route 53 weighted routing policy</em> is used to send a fixed percentage of traffic to specific endpoints based on assigned weights. This is typically used for gradual rollouts or testing, not for equal random distribution.</p><p><em>Launching three instances with two in one AZ and one in another</em> does not offer optimal fault tolerance. If the AZ with two instances fails, only one instance remains to handle all traffic, creating a performance bottleneck and a potential point of failure.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It evaluates your ability to design for high availability and fault tolerance using Route 53 and AZ-level redundancy—fundamental principles of resilient cloud architecture.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Distribute workloads across multiple AZs and use multivalue routing for basic load distribution without complex load balancers.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon Route 53 failover routing policy.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon Route 53 weighted routing policy.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon Route 53 multivalue answer routing policy.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Launch three EC2 instances: two instances in one Availability Zone and one instance in another Availability Zone.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Launch four EC2 instances: two instances in one Availability Zone and two instances in another Availability Zone.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 226,
  "query": "<p>A digital entertainment company currently collects and analyzes user engagement data using on-premises systems. They plan to migrate this data ingestion and analytics capability to AWS. The volume of data will continue to grow rapidly and is expected to reach petabyte scale. The company requires a highly available, low-maintenance solution that supports on-demand analytics using SQL queries on both newly ingested and historical data.</p><p>Which architecture will best fulfill these requirements with minimal operational burden?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Send activity data to an Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream and configure it to deliver data into an Amazon S3 bucket for on-demand SQL analytics.</strong></p><p>Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose is a fully managed service that reliably ingests streaming data and delivers it to destinations like Amazon S3 with minimal configuration or management. Once the data lands in S3, it can be queried on demand using Amazon Athena, which supports standard SQL queries over S3-stored data. This architecture supports large-scale ingestion, is fault tolerant, and eliminates the need to manage infrastructure manually.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Using Amazon Kinesis Data Streams to deliver data to S3</em> requires you to manage the data processing and delivery pipeline yourself—such as writing a consumer app or Lambda to move data to S3—which adds operational complexity compared to Firehose.</p><p><em>Using Kinesis Data Firehose to deliver directly to an Amazon Redshift cluster</em> introduces additional overhead. Redshift requires cluster management, scaling, and maintenance. While Redshift is powerful for analytics, S3 combined with Athena is a simpler and more scalable option for ad hoc queries on large datasets.</p><p><em>Configuring Amazon S3 to invoke a Lambda function on object arrival</em> is useful for light processing or triggers, but Lambda is not designed to handle continuous, high-throughput ingestion of petabyte-scale data efficiently.</p><p><em>Creating a custom ingestion layer on EC2 and writing to Amazon RDS</em> is operationally heavy, not horizontally scalable for massive data volumes, and RDS is not suited for petabyte-scale analytical workloads or SQL over unstructured/semi-structured event data.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It tests your understanding of scalable data ingestion architectures and your ability to design systems that balance performance, cost, and operational simplicity in AWS for big data and analytics scenarios.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When the question involves growing data volumes, ad hoc analytics, and SQL over raw data, think Amazon S3 + Athena as a foundational pattern. Kinesis Data Firehose is the easiest ingestion method to pair with this setup.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Send activity data to an Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose stream and configure it to deliver the data to an Amazon S3 bucket.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Send activity data to a Kinesis Data Firehose delivery stream and write directly to an Amazon Redshift cluster.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Place the activity data in an Amazon S3 bucket and configure S3 to invoke a Lambda function when data arrives.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Build a data ingestion service on Amazon EC2 instances across multiple Availability Zones and write data to an Amazon RDS Multi-AZ database.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 227,
  "query": "<p>A logistics company collects sensor data from thousands of IoT devices by using a RESTful web service hosted on a single Amazon EC2 instance. The instance transforms and stores the data in an Amazon S3 bucket. With rapid business expansion, the number of devices is expected to scale into the millions. The company wants to redesign the solution to handle increased load with minimal operational effort and maximum scalability.</p><p>Which combination of actions should the solutions architect implement to meet these goals? (Select two.)</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answers are:<br><strong>1. Use Amazon API Gateway to receive the raw data and forward it to an Amazon Kinesis data stream.<br>2. Configure Amazon Kinesis Data Firehose to use the stream as a source and deliver the data to Amazon S3.</strong></p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong><br>This approach enables a highly scalable and fully managed serverless data ingestion pipeline. Amazon API Gateway handles RESTful requests from millions of devices without the need to manage EC2 instances. The data is then streamed into Kinesis, which can buffer and batch the data before delivering it to S3 using Kinesis Data Firehose. This pattern is scalable, resilient, and requires minimal management overhead, perfectly matching the company's growth and operational goals.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Using AWS Glue to process raw data in S3</em> helps with transformation, but it doesn't address ingestion or scalability challenges. Glue is used for ETL, not real-time ingestion or front-line scaling.</p><p><em>Using Amazon Route 53 to distribute traffic to EC2 instances</em> is not ideal for REST API traffic or device-to-cloud communication. Route 53 is a DNS service, not a load balancer, and lacks the flexibility and control needed for application-level routing and scaling.</p><p><em>Adding more EC2 instances</em> increases operational overhead. Managing auto scaling groups, health checks, and load balancers requires more effort than using managed services like API Gateway and Kinesis, and it doesn't scale as seamlessly.</p><p><em>Sending raw data to SQS and using EC2 to process it</em> is more operationally heavy and doesn't scale as efficiently. While SQS is a good buffer, EC2 still introduces management overhead and is not as cost-effective as Kinesis Firehose for streaming ingestion into S3.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> This scenario assesses your ability to design scalable, serverless architectures using AWS managed services. It also tests your understanding of decoupling ingestion from processing to handle large volumes of data efficiently.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> For high-scale device data ingestion, prefer API Gateway + Kinesis + Firehose to eliminate EC2 dependencies and reduce operational complexity.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use AWS Glue to process the raw data in Amazon S3.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon Route 53 to route traffic to different EC2 instances.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Add more EC2 instances to accommodate the increasing amount of incoming data.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Send the raw data to Amazon API Gateway, then stream it into Amazon Kinesis. Use Kinesis Data Firehose to deliver data to Amazon S3.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Send the raw data to Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS). Use EC2 instances to process the data.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 227,
  "query": "<p>A company uses AWS Organizations to manage multiple accounts and enforces centralized logging through AWS CloudTrail from the management account. CloudTrail logs are delivered to a versioned Amazon S3 bucket that has a lifecycle policy configured to delete current object versions after 3 years. After more than 4 years of logging activity, the company notices that the total object count in the bucket continues to increase, even though the number of new logs remains steady.</p><p>Which action should the company take to ensure that all objects older than 3 years are deleted in the most cost-effective way?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Update the S3 Lifecycle configuration to also delete previous object versions in addition to current versions.</strong></p><p>S3 Versioning keeps all versions of an object, including deleted or replaced versions. If the lifecycle policy only deletes current versions, older noncurrent versions will accumulate over time. To reduce storage costs and enforce retention properly, the lifecycle policy must also include a rule to delete noncurrent (previous) versions after the retention period. This ensures that all object versions older than 3 years are removed automatically and efficiently.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Setting CloudTrail to expire objects</em> is not a valid feature. CloudTrail does not have a built-in expiration or deletion mechanism for S3 objects; retention is managed through S3 lifecycle policies.</p><p><em>Creating a Lambda function to manually delete old objects</em> is technically feasible but introduces unnecessary complexity and ongoing cost. S3 lifecycle policies are the native, low-cost, and automated way to manage object expiration.</p><p><em>Changing the object ownership to the parent account</em> does not affect object versioning or lifecycle management. S3 lifecycle policies apply regardless of which account owns the object, assuming proper permissions are in place.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It tests your understanding of how S3 Versioning and Lifecycle policies interact—especially in long-term logging or archival scenarios where storage costs and automated retention policies are critical.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Always check whether lifecycle rules are configured for both current and previous object versions when using versioned buckets, especially for log storage like CloudTrail.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Configure the organization’s centralized CloudTrail trail to expire objects after 3 years.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Configure the S3 Lifecycle policy to delete previous versions as well as current versions.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an AWS Lambda function to enumerate and delete objects from Amazon S3 that are older than 3 years.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Configure the parent account as the owner of all objects that are delivered to the S3 bucket.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 228,
  "query": "<p>A logistics company has developed an API that ingests real-time telemetry data from a network of monitoring devices. This API writes the incoming data directly to an Amazon RDS database for later analysis. The volume of data varies throughout the day. During traffic spikes, the API frequently returns timeout errors. After reviewing logs, the team finds that the database cannot handle the high rate of write requests, and the number of direct connections is overwhelming the DB instance.</p><p>What is the most effective solution to reduce database pressure, limit direct connections, and ensure that data is not lost during peak load periods?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Modify the API to send incoming data to an Amazon SQS queue. Configure an AWS Lambda function to be triggered by SQS and write the data to the database.</strong></p><p>This solution introduces a decoupled, buffer-based architecture. Amazon SQS serves as a highly durable and scalable queue that can absorb bursts of incoming traffic without overwhelming the database. By using a Lambda function to consume messages from the queue and write them to the RDS instance, the number of active connections to the database is reduced, and writes can be throttled to a manageable rate. This also helps eliminate timeout errors during spikes and ensures no data loss as messages are stored durably in SQS until processed.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Increasing the size of the RDS DB instance</em> might temporarily relieve pressure but does not address the root cause—connection saturation and variable traffic. It also increases costs and may not scale well during unpredictable surges.</p><p><em>Modifying the RDS instance to be Multi-AZ</em> only provides high availability and failover protection. It does not increase write throughput, nor does it allow writing to multiple RDS nodes concurrently.</p><p><em>Sending data to Amazon SNS and invoking Lambda from the SNS topic</em> is not ideal for this use case. SNS is designed for pub/sub messaging and broadcast delivery, not message queuing or traffic smoothing. Unlike SQS, SNS does not guarantee message ordering and is not well-suited for queue-based decoupling patterns.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It evaluates your ability to design a resilient and scalable ingestion pattern by introducing asynchronous processing and decoupling through AWS messaging services. Understanding when to use SQS versus SNS and how to manage database pressure are critical real-world design skills.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When facing high write volumes or fluctuating traffic, consider buffering with SQS to prevent database overload. SQS + Lambda is a cost-efficient and scalable pattern for smoothing workloads.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Increase the size of the RDS instance to a larger instance type with more memory.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Convert the RDS instance to Multi-AZ and configure the API to write to all available instances.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Update the API to publish data to an Amazon SQS queue. Use a Lambda function triggered by SQS to insert records into the RDS database.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Update the API to publish data to an Amazon SNS topic. Use a Lambda function subscribed to the topic to write to the database.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 229,
  "query": "<p>An e-commerce company currently runs its MySQL databases on Amazon EC2 instances. The team manually handles replication, scaling, and failover as traffic fluctuates. As the workload grows, these manual processes are becoming operationally burdensome. The company now wants to adopt a solution that will automatically adjust compute capacity based on demand while also improving performance, availability, and durability—all with minimal operational overhead.</p><p>Which approach best meets these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Migrate the databases to Amazon Aurora Serverless for Aurora MySQL.</strong></p><p>Amazon Aurora Serverless (for Aurora MySQL) is a fully managed database service that automatically scales compute capacity up or down based on traffic patterns. It removes the need to manage servers, replication, failover, or capacity planning. Aurora offers high performance and durability and is compatible with MySQL, making the migration process straightforward. It is specifically designed for workloads with unpredictable or variable traffic, which makes it ideal for this use case.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Migrating to Aurora Serverless for Aurora PostgreSQL</em> is not suitable because the current workloads are MySQL-based. Migrating to PostgreSQL would involve changing database engines and likely modifying application code, which contradicts the requirement for minimal effort.</p><p><em>Combining the databases into one larger MySQL database and running it on larger EC2 instances</em> increases complexity and risk. It does not solve the core problem of manual scaling and introduces a single point of failure with a large, monolithic instance.</p><p><em>Creating an EC2 Auto Scaling group for the database tier</em> is not feasible. Databases do not typically scale horizontally using EC2 Auto Scaling the way stateless applications do. You would still need to manually manage replication, synchronization, and failover between nodes.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It evaluates your ability to transition from traditional, self-managed environments to fully managed, serverless services that scale dynamically and reduce operational overhead. Recognizing when Aurora Serverless is appropriate is a key skill in modern cloud-native architecture.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When an organization wants to reduce database management overhead while gaining scalability and durability, Aurora Serverless is often the right solution—especially when staying within the same database engine family.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Migrate the databases to Amazon Aurora Serverless for Aurora MySQL.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Migrate the databases to Amazon Aurora Serverless for Aurora PostgreSQL.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Consolidate the databases into a larger MySQL instance hosted on EC2 and scale vertically.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use an EC2 Auto Scaling group to host the MySQL databases across multiple instances.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 230,
  "query": "<p>A financial services company is using two Amazon EC2 NAT instances to enable outbound internet access for resources in private subnets. As traffic increases, the company is concerned that these manually managed NAT instances may become a bottleneck and fail to scale with demand. The solution must offer high availability, fault tolerance, and automatic scaling with minimal operational overhead.</p><p>What should a solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Decommission the existing NAT instances and deploy two NAT gateways in separate Availability Zones.</strong></p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong><br>Amazon NAT Gateways are managed AWS services designed to provide scalable, highly available internet access for resources in private subnets. By placing NAT gateways in multiple Availability Zones, you achieve fault tolerance and high availability. AWS automatically handles scaling and redundancy, eliminating the need for manual instance management or load balancing.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Deploying two NAT gateways in the same Availability Zone</em> does not provide fault tolerance. If that AZ becomes unavailable, internet connectivity for all dependent resources is lost.</p><p><em>Using Auto Scaling groups with Network Load Balancers to manage EC2 NAT instances</em> increases complexity and requires manual configuration and management. While possible, it lacks the simplicity and automation of NAT gateways and still carries single points of failure without careful design.</p><p><em>Replacing NAT instances with EC2 Spot Instances</em> introduces risk, as Spot Instances can be terminated with little notice. This is inappropriate for critical infrastructure services like NAT, which must be stable and reliable.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It evaluates your knowledge of networking, availability, and managed service options in AWS. NAT Gateway is a common topic on the AWS exams and demonstrates how to improve reliability while reducing operational overhead.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> For highly available, scalable internet access from private subnets, always favor NAT Gateways in multiple AZs over EC2 NAT instances—especially as traffic grows.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Remove the existing NAT instances and deploy two NAT gateways in different Availability Zones.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Replace the two NAT instances with Spot Instances in different Availability Zones and use a Network Load Balancer.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Deploy two NAT gateways in the same Availability Zone to replace the current NAT instances.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Auto Scaling groups and Network Load Balancers to manage NAT instances across Availability Zones.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
