{
  "id": 231,
  "query": "<p>A company hosts an application on an Amazon EC2 instance within VPC A. The instance has an Elastic IP address. The application needs to connect securely to a database hosted in VPC B. Both VPCs are located in the same AWS account. The solution must allow secure private connectivity without exposing resources to the public internet.</p><p>What is the most secure way to enable communication between the EC2 instance in VPC A and the database in VPC B?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Establish a VPC peering connection between VPC A and VPC B.</strong></p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong><br>VPC peering enables private connectivity between two VPCs in the same or different AWS accounts. It allows traffic to flow between resources in both VPCs using private IP addresses without traversing the public internet. This is the most secure and efficient solution for enabling communication between an EC2 instance in one VPC and a database in another VPC within the same account. Once peered, you can configure route tables and security groups to allow only the necessary traffic (e.g., database port access).</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><p><em>Allowing all traffic from the EC2 instance's public IP</em> is not secure. This exposes the database to external IP-based access and forces traffic to route over the public internet, which violates security best practices.</p><p><em>Making the DB instance publicly accessible and assigning a public IP</em> significantly increases the attack surface. It is not necessary and is a poor design choice when both VPCs are in the same account and private connectivity options exist.</p><p><em>Creating a proxy EC2 instance in VPC B</em> adds unnecessary complexity, introduces a single point of failure, and does not improve security. It also requires you to manage and maintain an additional EC2 instance.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It tests your understanding of secure inter-VPC connectivity options and your ability to select the most appropriate networking solution that avoids the public internet.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When resources in different VPCs (but the same account or region) need private communication, VPC peering is usually the simplest and most secure choice unless there are overlapping CIDR blocks or more complex routing needs, in which case Transit Gateway may be better.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create a DB instance security group that allows all traffic from the public IP address of the EC2 instance in VPC A.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Configure a VPC peering connection between VPC A and VPC B.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Make the database publicly accessible and assign it a public IP address.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Deploy an EC2 instance with an Elastic IP in VPC B to act as a proxy for database access.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 232,
  "query": "<p>A company hosts customer demonstration environments using Amazon EC2 instances. Each demo environment is deployed in a separate VPC. The operations team requires immediate notification whenever a user establishes an RDP or SSH connection to one of these EC2 instances.</p><p>Which solution will provide this functionality with minimal effort and support near real-time notifications?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is: <strong>Publish VPC flow logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. Create required metric filters. Create an Amazon CloudWatch metric alarm with a notification action for when the alarm is in the ALARM state.</strong></p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong><br>VPC flow logs capture IP traffic data to and from network interfaces in your VPCs. By publishing these logs to CloudWatch Logs, you can use metric filters to detect traffic patterns—such as established SSH (port 22) or RDP (port 3389) connections. You can then configure a CloudWatch alarm to trigger when such access is detected and send notifications via Amazon SNS to the operations team. This is a scalable, near-real-time, and low-maintenance solution.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><em>Amazon CloudWatch Application Insights</em> is used for monitoring and diagnostics of application performance and health, not for detecting RDP/SSH access specifically.</li><li><em>Attaching an IAM role with AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore</em> enables Systems Manager access but does not track or alert on network-level RDP/SSH logins.</li><li><em>Amazon EventBridge rules for EC2 state-change notifications</em> can notify on instance lifecycle events (like start, stop, terminate) but cannot detect interactive access (like SSH or RDP sessions).</li></ul><p><strong>Tip:</strong> SSH and RDP sessions leave identifiable patterns in VPC flow logs, making them ideal for detection using log-based monitoring.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Configure Amazon CloudWatch Application Insights to create AWS Systems Manager OpsItems when RDP or SSH access is detected.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Configure the EC2 instances with an IAM instance profile that has an IAM role with the AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore policy attached.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Publish VPC flow logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs. Create required metric filters. Create an Amazon CloudWatch metric alarm with a notification action for when the alarm is in the ALARM state.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Configure an Amazon EventBridge rule to listen for events of type EC2 Instance State-change Notification. Configure an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic as a target. Subscribe the operations team to the topic.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 233,
  "query": "<p>A solutions architect has created a new AWS account and must secure the AWS account root user access.</p><p>Which <strong>two</strong> actions should the solutions architect take to achieve this? (Choose two.)</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answers are:</p><ul><li><strong>Ensure the root user uses a strong password.</strong></li><li><strong>Enable multi-factor authentication to the root user.</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Explanation:</strong><br>The root user has full access to all resources in an AWS account, so it's essential to secure it. AWS recommends the following best practices for root user access:</p><ul><li><strong>Use a strong password</strong> to reduce the risk of brute-force or credential-stuffing attacks.</li><li><strong>Enable multi-factor authentication (MFA)</strong> to provide an additional layer of security in case credentials are compromised.</li></ul><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><em>Storing root user access keys in an encrypted S3 bucket</em> still poses a security risk and is discouraged. Best practice is to avoid creating access keys for the root user altogether.</li><li><em>Adding the root user to a group</em> is not possible. IAM groups are for IAM users only; the root user is not managed like an IAM identity.</li><li><em>Applying inline policies to the root user</em> is also not applicable. The root user inherently has full access and cannot have IAM policies attached to it.</li></ul>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Ensure the root user uses a strong password.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Enable multi-factor authentication to the root user.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Store root user access keys in an encrypted Amazon S3 bucket.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Add the root user to a group containing administrative permissions.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Apply the required permissions to the root user with an inline policy document.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
