{
  "id": 235,
  "query": "<p>A company is migrating its on-premises Oracle database to Amazon Aurora PostgreSQL. The database is accessed by multiple applications that write to the same tables. These applications will be moved one at a time, with a month between each migration. Management is concerned about the database’s high volume of read and write operations. During the migration, the data must remain synchronized between the source Oracle database and the target Aurora PostgreSQL database.</p><p>What should a solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements? (Choose 1)</p>",
  "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong><br><strong>Use the AWS Schema Conversion Tool with AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) using a memory optimized replication instance. Create a full load plus change data capture (CDC) replication task and a table mapping to select all tables.</strong></p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong><br>The AWS Schema Conversion Tool is used to convert the Oracle schema to a PostgreSQL-compatible format. AWS DMS supports a <em>full load plus change data capture (CDC)</em> mode, which performs an initial full load and then keeps the target database synchronized by capturing ongoing changes. A <strong>memory-optimized replication instance</strong> is ideal for environments with high write throughput, ensuring replication performance during the hybrid phase where both databases remain in use.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><em>Using AWS DataSync for the initial migration</em>: DataSync is not designed for live, ongoing database replication. It is optimized for file-based transfers, not transactional databases.</li><li><em>Using a compute-optimized replication instance</em>: Compute-optimized instances may not provide sufficient memory buffering and I/O performance for write-heavy CDC operations.</li><li><em>Selecting only the largest tables</em>: This would risk leaving important changes unsynchronized. All tables must be kept in sync for consistency during phased application migration.</li></ul>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use AWS DataSync for the initial migration. Use AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) to create a change data capture (CDC) replication task and a table mapping to select all tables.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use AWS DataSync for the initial migration. Use AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) to create a full load plus change data capture (CDC) replication task and a table mapping to select all tables.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use the AWS Schema Conversion Tool with AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) using a memory optimized replication instance. Create a full load plus change data capture (CDC) replication task and a table mapping to select all tables.",
      "correct": true,
