{
  "id": 236,
  "query": "<p>A company has a three-tier application for image sharing. The application uses an Amazon EC2 instance for the front-end layer, another EC2 instance for the application layer, and a third EC2 instance for a MySQL database. A solutions architect must design a scalable and highly available solution that requires the least amount of change to the application.</p><p>Which solution meets these requirements? (Choose 1)</p>",
  "answer": "<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong><br><strong>Use load-balanced Multi-AZ AWS Elastic Beanstalk environments for the front-end layer and the application layer. Move the database to an Amazon RDS Multi-AZ DB instance. Use Amazon S3 to store and serve users’ images.</strong></p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong><br>This option offers high availability and scalability with minimal code change. AWS Elastic Beanstalk supports deployment of existing applications with minimal configuration and includes load balancing and Auto Scaling by default. Amazon RDS Multi-AZ provides a managed, fault-tolerant MySQL solution. Using Amazon S3 for image storage offloads static content delivery, improving performance and scalability.</p><p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p><ul><li><em>S3 hosting for frontend, Lambda for application logic, and DynamoDB</em>: This would require substantial reengineering, violating the constraint to minimize application changes.</li><li><em>Elastic Beanstalk for frontend/app, RDS with multiple read replicas</em>: While valid, read replicas serve read traffic but do not improve write availability. Multi-AZ RDS better addresses high availability for both read and write operations.</li><li><em>S3 frontend hosting with Auto Scaling group and memory-optimized DB</em>: S3 can only serve static sites. The use of memory-optimized DB may not directly improve availability and doesn't provide managed failover.</li></ul>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon S3 to host the front-end layer. Use AWS Lambda functions for the application layer. Move the database to an Amazon DynamoDB table. Use Amazon S3 to store and serve users’ images.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use load-balanced Multi-AZ AWS Elastic Beanstalk environments for the front-end layer and the application layer. Move the database to an Amazon RDS DB instance with multiple read replicas to serve users’ images.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon S3 to host the front-end layer. Use a fleet of EC2 instances in an Auto Scaling group for the application layer. Move the database to a memory optimized instance type to store and serve users’ images.",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
