{
  "id": 237,
  "query": "<p>A media production firm has an application deployed on an Amazon EC2 instance located in a VPC within Account Alpha. This application must retrieve video files hosted on another EC2 instance that resides in a different VPC managed under Account Beta. The company's network engineer has been asked to implement a solution that provides secure, scalable connectivity between these VPCs without introducing a single point of failure or risking bandwidth limitations. What is the most effective way to establish this connection?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Set up a VPC peering connection between the VPCs in Account Alpha and Account Beta</strong>.</p><p>VPC peering is a networking connection between two VPCs that enables traffic to route privately between them using private IP addresses. It is designed to be both secure and highly available, as it does not depend on a gateway, VPN, or physical device, which eliminates any single point of failure. Additionally, VPC peering supports high-bandwidth traffic because it leverages the AWS global network backbone without throttling like some other VPN-based solutions.</p><p>Let’s examine why the other choices are incorrect:</p><p><em>Set up VPC gateway endpoints for the EC2 instance in Account Beta</em> is not valid in this scenario. Gateway endpoints are used to connect to AWS services like S3 or DynamoDB, not to connect two EC2 instances across VPCs.</p><p><em>Attach a virtual private gateway to VPC-B and configure routes from VPC-A</em> describes a VPN-like architecture, which typically involves a customer gateway and is more appropriate for connecting on-premises networks to AWS—not for connecting two AWS-managed VPCs across accounts.</p><p><em>Create a private virtual interface (VIF) for the EC2 instance in VPC-B and add routes from VPC-A</em> refers to Direct Connect configurations, which are usually employed for high-throughput, low-latency connections between AWS and on-premises networks. Using a private VIF for EC2-to-EC2 communication across VPCs within AWS is overkill and more complex than necessary.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It assesses the candidate's understanding of cross-account VPC connectivity strategies, and specifically whether they can distinguish between use cases for VPC peering, Direct Connect, VPNs, and gateway endpoints. A good strategy for answering questions like this is to focus on the use case (VPC-to-VPC, secure, scalable, AWS-internal) and match it with the simplest AWS-native solution that meets the technical and operational constraints.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Set up a VPC peering connection between the VPCs in Account Alpha and Account Beta",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Set up VPC gateway endpoints in the VPC for the EC2 instance in Account Beta",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Attach a virtual private gateway to the VPC in Account Beta and route traffic from the VPC in Account Alpha through it",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a private virtual interface (VIF) for the EC2 instance in Account Beta and configure route tables in Account Alpha",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 238,
  "query": "<p>A startup specializing in AI research has decided to provide each engineer with a separate AWS account to promote experimentation and sandbox testing. The finance team wants to ensure that EC2 spending stays within budget across these accounts. Specifically, they need an automated, cost-efficient way to receive notifications whenever EC2 usage costs exceed a defined monthly threshold in any individual account. What is the most suitable and economical solution to implement this requirement?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Use AWS Budgets to create a cost budget for each account. Set the period to monthly. Set the scope to EC2 instances. Set an alert threshold for the budget. Configure an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic to receive a notification when a threshold is exceeded</strong>.</p><p>AWS Budgets is the most appropriate and cost-effective tool for this use case. It allows you to define monthly budgets by service (such as EC2), and configure alerts based on spending thresholds. The service integrates natively with Amazon SNS, enabling automated email or SMS notifications. Budgets are easy to set up on a per-account basis and provide precise control over cost monitoring with minimal overhead.</p><p><em>Use Cost Explorer to create a daily report of costs by service. Filter the report by EC2 instances. Configure Cost Explorer to send an Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES) notification when a threshold is exceeded</em> is incorrect because Cost Explorer is primarily a visual reporting tool and doesn’t support automated threshold-based notifications with SES.</p><p><em>Use Cost Explorer to create a monthly report of costs by service. Filter the report by EC2 instances. Configure Cost Explorer to send an Amazon Simple Email Service (Amazon SES) notification when a threshold is exceeded</em> is also incorrect for the same reason—Cost Explorer does not provide automated cost alerting via SES. It’s useful for analysis, not monitoring or alerts.</p><p><em>Use AWS Cost and Usage Reports to create a report with hourly granularity. Integrate the report data with Amazon Athena. Use Amazon EventBridge to schedule an Athena query. Configure an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic to receive a notification when a threshold is exceeded</em> is technically possible but overly complex and expensive for the use case described. While powerful, this approach involves managing Athena queries, EventBridge schedules, and detailed report parsing, which is excessive for basic budget threshold monitoring.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your ability to match a business requirement (cost monitoring and notification) with the right AWS service while considering simplicity and cost. AWS Budgets is purpose-built for this kind of alerting, and recognizing the difference between reporting tools and monitoring tools is key.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When budget thresholds and alerts are mentioned in a question, always consider AWS Budgets first before diving into more complex reporting or data processing pipelines.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use AWS Budgets to create a cost budget for each account. Set the period to monthly. Set the scope to EC2 instances. Set an alert threshold for the budget. Configure an Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) topic to receive a notification when a threshold is exceeded",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
