{
  "id": 239,
  "query": "<p>An e-learning company is developing a new microservice that students will access through HTTPS. The microservice will be built using a single AWS Lambda function written in Go 1.x and must authenticate incoming requests using AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM). A solutions architect needs to select the most operationally efficient deployment strategy that satisfies the HTTPS requirement and integrates IAM-based authentication. What is the best solution to meet these goals?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Create a Lambda function URL for the function. Specify AWS_IAM as the authentication type</strong>.</p><p>Lambda function URLs provide a built-in HTTPS endpoint for a Lambda function, allowing clients to invoke the function directly without the need to configure API Gateway or other HTTP proxies. You can configure the function URL to use <strong>AWS_IAM</strong> as the authentication mechanism, which means requests must be signed with AWS credentials and IAM policies will determine access—exactly what is required by the scenario. This approach minimizes operational overhead by avoiding the need for additional services and configurations, making it the most efficient solution.</p><p><em>Create an Amazon API Gateway REST API. Configure the method to use the Lambda function. Enable IAM authentication on the API</em> is a valid solution but involves more operational complexity and cost than necessary. While API Gateway does support IAM-based authentication, it also requires additional configuration and management compared to Lambda function URLs.</p><p><em>Create an Amazon CloudFront distribution. Deploy the function to Lambda@Edge. Integrate IAM authentication logic into the Lambda@Edge function</em> is incorrect because Lambda@Edge does not natively support IAM authentication for HTTPS requests. Additionally, using Lambda@Edge for basic HTTPS invocation adds unnecessary complexity and global propagation time.</p><p><em>Create an Amazon CloudFront distribution. Deploy the function to CloudFront Functions. Specify AWS_IAM as the authentication type</em> is incorrect because CloudFront Functions are limited in capabilities and do not support integration with IAM authentication mechanisms. Also, they are not designed to replace full Lambda-based application logic.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your knowledge of modern AWS serverless options and your ability to select the simplest and most cost-effective solution. Lambda function URLs are a relatively new feature that reduce the need for API Gateway in simple scenarios. Recognizing when to use them can lead to more efficient architectures.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When you see a question about exposing a Lambda function through HTTPS with IAM authentication and minimal overhead, consider Lambda function URLs with <code>AWS_IAM</code> as your first option.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create a Lambda function URL for the function. Specify AWS_IAM as the authentication type",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon API Gateway REST API. Configure the method to use the Lambda function. Enable IAM authentication on the API",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon CloudFront distribution. Deploy the function to Lambda@Edge. Integrate IAM authentication logic into the Lambda@Edge function",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon CloudFront distribution. Deploy the function to CloudFront Functions. Specify AWS_IAM as the authentication type",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
}, 
{
  "id": 240,
  "query": "<p>An analytics firm has moved its data warehousing infrastructure to AWS. The company also has an AWS Direct Connect connection in place. Employees at the head office use a browser-based dashboarding tool to run analytical queries against the data warehouse. On average, each query result is 50 MB in size, while the corresponding web page response from the dashboard tool is about 500 KB. The data warehouse does not cache query results. What is the most cost-efficient approach to minimize data transfer (egress) charges from AWS?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Host the visualization tool in the same AWS Region as the data warehouse and access it over a Direct Connect connection at a location in the same Region</strong>.</p><p>This setup ensures that large query results (50 MB per request) are transferred internally within the same Region in AWS, avoiding internet-based data transfer charges. Then, only the small, processed visualization output (~500 KB) needs to traverse the Direct Connect link to on-premises users. Since Direct Connect offers lower and more predictable egress pricing than standard internet data transfer, and same-Region Direct Connect transfers are especially cost-effective, this setup provides the lowest data transfer costs overall.</p><p><em>Host the visualization tool on premises and query the data warehouse directly over the internet</em> is the most expensive option. Data transfer out to the internet from AWS incurs the highest rates, especially for large payloads like the 50 MB query results being returned.</p><p><em>Host the visualization tool in the same AWS Region as the data warehouse. Access it over the internet</em> avoids AWS-internal transfer costs between the tool and the data warehouse but still sends the large 50 MB result sets to on-premises users via the public internet—again incurring higher egress fees.</p><p><em>Host the visualization tool on premises and query the data warehouse directly over a Direct Connect connection at a location in the same AWS Region</em> improves cost compared to internet-based transfer, but still requires sending the full 50 MB query responses over Direct Connect. While cheaper than internet, this is still more costly than sending only the 500 KB post-processed visualization output if the tool is hosted in-region.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your ability to reduce operational costs by strategically placing compute resources to minimize expensive data transfers. Understanding how data moves—especially across AWS services, Regions, Direct Connect links, and the internet—is critical for cost-efficient cloud design.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When AWS Direct Connect is involved, always ask: where is the bulk of the data traveling? Hosting compute close to where large data lives can lead to substantial cost savings.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Host the visualization tool in the same AWS Region as the data warehouse and access it over a Direct Connect connection at a location in the same Region",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Host the visualization tool on premises and query the data warehouse directly over the internet",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Host the visualization tool on premises and query the data warehouse directly over a Direct Connect connection at a location in the same AWS Region",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Host the visualization tool in the same AWS Region as the data warehouse. Access it over the internet",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
