{
  "id": 241,
  "query": "<p>A fintech startup is transitioning its operations to AWS. The company stores customer transaction data in a PostgreSQL database. To meet business continuity goals, the company requires that its data remain accessible and live across multiple AWS Regions, even during a Regional outage. Which approach offers the required multi-Region availability with the least operational overhead?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Migrate the PostgreSQL database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance. Create a read replica in another Region</strong>.</p><p>This solution provides asynchronous cross-Region replication using Amazon RDS, which ensures that customer data is continually duplicated to another AWS Region. In the event of a failure in the primary Region, the read replica can be promoted to a standalone database instance. This setup minimizes management burden while satisfying the requirement for multi-Region availability and disaster recovery.</p><p><em>Migrate the PostgreSQL database to a PostgreSQL cluster on Amazon EC2 instances</em> offers control but adds significant operational complexity. You are responsible for managing updates, replication, failover, and network security across Regions.</p><p><em>Migrate the PostgreSQL database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance with the Multi-AZ feature turned on</em> improves fault tolerance within a single Region but does not provide cross-Region resilience, which disqualifies it from meeting the scenario’s requirements.</p><p><em>Migrate the PostgreSQL database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance. Set up DB snapshots to be copied to another Region</em> enables backup-based disaster recovery but does not ensure continuous availability. Snapshots are point-in-time and require manual recovery, which introduces downtime.</p><p><strong>Why this question is important:</strong> It evaluates your knowledge of designing for fault tolerance and high availability across Regions. Knowing when to use read replicas, Multi-AZ, and backups is key for architecting resilient systems on AWS.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> If the question emphasizes automatic availability in multiple Regions with minimal effort, cross-Region read replicas on Amazon RDS are often the best-fit answer.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Migrate the PostgreSQL database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance. Create a read replica in another Region",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Migrate the PostgreSQL database to a PostgreSQL cluster on Amazon EC2 instances",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Migrate the PostgreSQL database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance with the Multi-AZ feature turned on",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Migrate the PostgreSQL database to an Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance. Set up DB snapshots to be copied to another Region",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 242,
  "query": "<p>A digital publishing company runs its content delivery platform on AWS using a fleet of seven Amazon EC2 instances. The company wants to ensure that every DNS query returns the IP addresses of all EC2 instances that are currently healthy, so that client devices can perform their own load balancing. What Route 53 routing policy should be applied to meet this requirement?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Multivalue routing policy</strong>.</p><p>Amazon Route 53’s <strong>Multivalue routing policy</strong> is specifically designed for scenarios where you want DNS to return multiple values—typically IP addresses—associated with healthy resources. It also integrates optional health checks, so only healthy IPs are included in the response. This allows client-side load balancing by returning up to eight healthy records per query, making it the best match for the scenario where all healthy EC2 IPs need to be included in DNS responses.</p><p><em>Simple routing policy</em> returns a single value, without regard for health status or multiple endpoints. It cannot meet the requirement to return all healthy IPs.</p><p><em>Latency routing policy</em> directs traffic to the Region with the lowest latency based on the user's location. It is used for performance optimization, not for returning all healthy endpoints.</p><p><em>Geolocation routing policy</em> responds to DNS queries based on the geographic location of the user making the request. It does not return multiple IPs nor does it focus on endpoint health.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your understanding of Amazon Route 53 routing policies and their appropriate use cases. Knowing the differences between policies such as Simple, Latency, Geolocation, and Multivalue is critical in designing DNS strategies that balance availability, performance, and user experience.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When a question mentions returning multiple healthy IP addresses or performing basic client-side load balancing through DNS, Multivalue routing is often the correct choice.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Multivalue routing policy",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Simple routing policy",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Latency routing policy",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Geolocation routing policy",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 243,
  "query": "<p>A national healthcare research institute generates data files related to clinical trials. These files need to be accessed with low latency by hospitals and clinics across the country. Each clinic uses on-premises, file-based software that requires direct access to the data. The research data is stored centrally in an Amazon S3 bucket, and each clinic has read-only access permissions. What is the most effective solution to provide quick and seamless access to this data from each clinic's on-premises environment?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Deploy an AWS Storage Gateway file gateway as a virtual machine (VM) on premises at each clinic</strong>.</p><p>AWS Storage Gateway file gateway provides an NFS or SMB interface for on-premises applications, allowing them to access objects in Amazon S3 as if they were files on a local file system. This approach enables seamless integration with existing file-based applications, while keeping the data in S3. It also supports local caching, which improves performance and minimizes latency when accessing frequently used data.</p><p><em>Migrate the files to each clinic’s on-premises applications by using AWS DataSync for processing</em> is not the best option. DataSync is optimized for bulk, scheduled, or one-time transfers and doesn’t provide ongoing, seamless integration with on-premises file systems. It also doesn't provide real-time access or local caching.</p><p><em>Deploy an AWS Storage Gateway volume gateway as a virtual machine (VM) on premises at each clinic</em> is incorrect because volume gateways expose iSCSI block volumes, not file shares. These are suited for block storage use cases, not file-based applications that expect standard file protocols.</p><p><em>Attach an Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) file system to each clinic’s on-premises servers</em> is not viable because EFS is only directly accessible from AWS environments or on-premises environments using AWS Direct Connect or VPN, which adds complexity and may not meet the low-latency requirement without significant setup.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your understanding of hybrid storage solutions and the ability to provide low-latency access to cloud-stored data from on-premises systems. Knowing the difference between file, block, and object storage access models is essential when selecting the right AWS storage service for enterprise integration.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When you see a requirement for file-based access to S3-stored data from on-premises environments, especially with low latency and read-only permissions, AWS Storage Gateway with a file gateway is usually the best match.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Deploy an AWS Storage Gateway file gateway as a virtual machine (VM) on premises at each clinic",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Migrate the files to each clinic’s on-premises applications by using AWS DataSync for processing",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Deploy an AWS Storage Gateway volume gateway as a virtual machine (VM) on premises at each clinic",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Attach an Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS) file system to each clinic’s on-premises servers",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
