  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 244,
  "query": "<p>A small media company runs a content management system on a single Amazon EC2 instance. This instance hosts both the web server and the backend database. To improve reliability and accommodate future growth in web traffic, the company wants to redesign the architecture for high availability and scalability. What solution should a solutions architect recommend to achieve these goals?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Move the database to Amazon Aurora with a read replica in another Availability Zone. Create an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) from the EC2 instance. Configure an Application Load Balancer in two Availability Zones. Attach an Auto Scaling group that uses the AMI across two Availability Zones</strong>.</p><p>This solution decouples the web and database layers, placing the database in a managed service (Aurora), which provides automatic failover, high availability, and the ability to scale reads using replicas. It also improves fault tolerance by placing web servers behind a load balancer across multiple Availability Zones and automating scaling through an Auto Scaling group. This design ensures that both web and database components can survive instance or AZ failures and scale with user demand.</p><p><em>Move the database to Amazon RDS, and enable automatic backups. Manually launch another EC2 instance in the same Availability Zone. Configure an Application Load Balancer in the Availability Zone, and set the two instances as targets</em> is insufficient because all resources remain in a single Availability Zone, making the setup vulnerable to AZ failures. Also, manual instance management lacks automation and scalability.</p><p><em>Migrate the database to an Amazon Aurora instance with a read replica in the same Availability Zone as the existing EC2 instance. Manually launch another EC2 instance in the same Availability Zone. Configure an Application Load Balancer, and set the two EC2 instances as targets</em> introduces some database scaling but fails to provide high availability across AZs. Like the previous option, it also lacks automated scaling.</p><p><em>Move the database to a separate EC2 instance, and schedule backups to Amazon S3. Create an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) from the original EC2 instance. Configure an Application Load Balancer in two Availability Zones. Attach an Auto Scaling group that uses the AMI across two Availability Zones</em> is better for the web tier, but keeping the database on EC2 instead of a managed service like Aurora introduces more operational burden and potential single points of failure. Backups to S3 do not provide immediate failover.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your ability to design fault-tolerant, scalable architectures using AWS managed services and multi-AZ deployments. High availability requires spreading resources across Availability Zones, and scalability often means combining load balancing with Auto Scaling and decoupling the database layer.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Look for managed database services like RDS or Aurora when high availability and scalability are required. Combine that with multi-AZ load balancing and Auto Scaling to fully meet resilience and performance goals.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
