      "text": "Update the route tables for the EC2 instances’ subnets to send 0.0.0.0/0 traffic through the internet gateway route. Add a rule to the EC2 instances’ security groups to allow outbound traffic to 0.0.0.0/0",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 247,
  "query": "<p>An e-commerce platform is experiencing increased database traffic on its MySQL database hosted on Amazon RDS. The support team has noticed degraded read performance and suggests that a read replica should be added to help offload traffic. Before proceeding with the deployment of a read replica, which two steps must a solutions architect ensure are completed?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answers are <strong>Enable automatic backups on the source instance by setting the backup retention period to a value other than 0</strong> and <strong>Enable binlog replication on the RDS primary node</strong>.</p><p>To create an Amazon RDS read replica for a MySQL database, the source DB instance must have automated backups enabled. This is done by setting the backup retention period to a value greater than zero. Without this, replication cannot be initialized. Additionally, binary logging (binlog replication) must be enabled on the source database to allow changes to be captured and streamed to the replica.</p><p><em>Choose a failover priority for the source DB instance</em> is not required for creating a read replica. Failover priority is a setting used with Amazon Aurora or Multi-AZ deployments and is unrelated to read replica configuration.</p><p><em>Allow long-running transactions to complete on the source DB instance</em> is not a prerequisite for creating a read replica. While it may help avoid replication lag, it is not a technical requirement for initiating replication.</p><p><em>Create a global table and specify the AWS Regions where the table will be available</em> applies to Amazon DynamoDB and is completely unrelated to Amazon RDS or MySQL.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests knowledge of prerequisites for deploying read replicas in Amazon RDS, which is a critical strategy for improving read scalability and offloading query traffic. These configuration steps are often overlooked by those unfamiliar with the operational aspects of RDS replication.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Always ensure automated backups are enabled and that binary logging is turned on before trying to create a read replica in Amazon RDS for MySQL. These are mandatory steps and will block the replica creation process if not configured correctly.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Enable binlog replication on the RDS primary node",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Choose a failover priority for the source DB instance",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Allow long-running transactions to complete on the source DB instance",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a global table and specify the AWS Regions where the table will be available",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Enable automatic backups on the source instance by setting the backup retention period to a value other than 0",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 248,
  "query": "<p>A scientific research company operates data processing software on Amazon EC2 instances. Researchers upload raw datasets to Amazon S3 and submit jobs for analysis. Recently, users have reported that some datasets are not being processed. Amazon CloudWatch metrics show that the EC2 instances consistently operate at or near 100% CPU utilization. The company wants a scalable solution that improves processing performance and handles variable user demand. What should a solutions architect recommend to address this issue?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Route incoming requests to Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS). Configure an EC2 Auto Scaling group based on queue size. Update the software to read from the queue</strong>.</p><p>This architecture decouples job submission from processing. By placing job requests into an SQS queue, you ensure that none are lost during peak load. Auto Scaling can then launch additional EC2 instances based on the number of pending jobs in the queue. This setup allows the system to dynamically adapt to changes in workload while reducing CPU bottlenecks and improving reliability and performance.</p><p><em>Create a copy of the instance. Place all instances behind an Application Load Balancer</em> might help distribute incoming requests, but it doesn't automatically scale based on demand or address the CPU saturation issue without Auto Scaling. Additionally, ALBs are better suited for stateless HTTP traffic, not job queuing or processing scenarios.</p><p><em>Create an S3 VPC endpoint for Amazon S3. Update the software to reference the endpoint</em> improves security and performance of S3 access inside a VPC, but it doesn’t solve the root problem—CPU overload and lack of workload scaling.</p><p><em>Stop the EC2 instances. Modify the instance type to one with a more powerful CPU and more memory. Restart the instances</em> may temporarily improve performance, but it doesn’t provide elasticity. Without Auto Scaling, the system won’t adjust to future traffic spikes and remains vulnerable to overload.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your understanding of how to build scalable and fault-tolerant processing architectures using Amazon SQS and EC2 Auto Scaling. This design pattern is commonly used to decouple workloads and ensure no job is lost under load.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When you see 100% CPU utilization and dropped or missed jobs, look for decoupling patterns using SQS, Lambda, or Auto Scaling. These allow the system to scale based on real-time demand and improve overall resilience.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Route incoming requests to Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS). Configure an EC2 Auto Scaling group based on queue size. Update the software to read from the queue",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a copy of the instance. Place all instances behind an Application Load Balancer",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an S3 VPC endpoint for Amazon S3. Update the software to reference the endpoint",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Stop the EC2 instances. Modify the instance type to one with a more powerful CPU and more memory. Restart the instances",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 249,
  "query": "<p>A digital content production company is deploying a media editing application in the AWS Cloud. The application requires shared file storage that can be accessed by multiple clients using the SMB protocol. The company prefers a fully managed solution that minimizes administrative overhead and scales easily with demand. Which AWS service should the solutions architect recommend?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Create an Amazon FSx for Windows File Server file system. Attach the file system to the origin server. Connect the application server to the file system</strong>.</p><p>Amazon FSx for Windows File Server is a fully managed native Windows file system that supports the Server Message Block (SMB) protocol. It integrates with Microsoft Active Directory and is designed specifically for Windows-based applications that require shared storage. It is fully managed by AWS, eliminating the need to handle hardware provisioning, patching, or scaling manually. This makes it ideal for use cases like media production, where SMB support and low operational overhead are key requirements.</p><p><em>Create an AWS Storage Gateway volume gateway. Create a file share that uses the required client protocol. Connect the application server to the file share</em> is incorrect because the volume gateway presents block storage over iSCSI, not SMB. It is not suitable for shared file access via SMB clients.</p><p><em>Create an AWS Storage Gateway tape gateway. Configure tapes to use Amazon S3. Connect the application server to the tape gateway</em> is unrelated to the use case. Tape gateways are intended for backup workflows that simulate physical tape libraries, not for active shared file storage.</p><p><em>Create an Amazon EC2 Windows instance. Install and configure a Windows file share role on the instance. Connect the application server to the file share</em> technically supports SMB, but it requires significant manual configuration, maintenance, and monitoring, which violates the requirement for a fully managed solution.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It assesses your ability to choose the appropriate AWS storage service for SMB-based workloads, particularly in media and content collaboration scenarios. It also tests your understanding of managed service offerings vs. self-managed alternatives.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When the scenario calls for SMB support and shared file access with minimal management, Amazon FSx for Windows File Server should be your default go-to option.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon FSx for Windows File Server file system. Attach the file system to the origin server. Connect the application server to the file system",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an AWS Storage Gateway volume gateway. Create a file share that uses the required client protocol. Connect the application server to the file share",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an AWS Storage Gateway tape gateway. Configure tapes to use Amazon S3. Connect the application server to the tape gateway",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an Amazon EC2 Windows instance. Install and configure a Windows file share role on the instance. Connect the application server to the file share",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
