{
  "id": 250,
  "query": "<p>An enterprise IT department has mandated that all network activity within their AWS VPC be logged for security auditing. These VPC Flow Logs must be retained and frequently reviewed for the first 90 days and then stored for occasional access afterward. A solutions architect needs to design a logging solution that meets the access pattern and optimizes storage costs. What is the best configuration to meet these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Use Amazon S3 as the target. Enable an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition the logs to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 90 days</strong>.</p><p>Storing VPC Flow Logs in Amazon S3 allows for durable, scalable storage, and integration with lifecycle management. By keeping logs in the S3 Standard storage class for the first 90 days, they remain readily accessible. After 90 days, a lifecycle policy can transition them to S3 Standard-IA, which is more cost-effective for infrequently accessed data while still offering millisecond retrieval. This approach balances performance with cost optimization.</p><p><em>Use Amazon CloudWatch as the target. Set the CloudWatch log group with an expiration of 90 days</em> would delete the logs after 90 days, which violates the requirement for long-term intermittent access.</p><p><em>Use Amazon Kinesis as the target. Configure the Kinesis stream to always retain the logs for 90 days</em> is incorrect because Kinesis is designed for real-time data streaming and short-term retention, not long-term log storage or intermittent access. It also incurs higher costs for extended retention.</p><p><em>Use AWS CloudTrail as the target. Configure CloudTrail to save to an Amazon S3 bucket, and enable S3 Intelligent-Tiering</em> is misleading. CloudTrail is not a valid target for VPC Flow Logs, and Intelligent-Tiering is best suited for unpredictable access patterns—not for predictable transitions after a fixed period like 90 days.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It evaluates your ability to architect cost-effective, long-term storage solutions for audit and compliance logs using AWS-native tools like S3 and lifecycle management. Understanding storage classes and retention strategies is essential for cloud efficiency and compliance.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> If you see a predictable pattern of high-to-low access over time, S3 lifecycle policies paired with Standard and Standard-IA classes are a smart choice. Don’t confuse log storage targets—CloudWatch and CloudTrail are not interchangeable with VPC Flow Logs.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon S3 as the target. Enable an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition the logs to S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) after 90 days",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon CloudWatch as the target. Set the CloudWatch log group with an expiration of 90 days",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use Amazon Kinesis as the target. Configure the Kinesis stream to always retain the logs for 90 days",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Use AWS CloudTrail as the target. Configure CloudTrail to save to an Amazon S3 bucket, and enable S3 Intelligent-Tiering",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 251,
  "query": "<p>An EC2 instance has been provisioned inside a private subnet of a newly created VPC. The subnet currently lacks any form of outbound internet access. However, the EC2 instance must be able to retrieve monthly security patches from an external vendor's internet-facing service. What is the appropriate solution to allow the EC2 instance to securely download these updates without exposing it directly to the internet?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Create a NAT gateway, and place it in a public subnet. Configure the private subnet route table to use the NAT gateway as the default route</strong>.</p><p>This solution enables instances in private subnets to initiate outbound connections to the internet (for downloads, updates, etc.) while still blocking unsolicited inbound connections. A NAT gateway is a fully managed service by AWS that supports high availability and scalability, and is the preferred approach over NAT instances for most use cases.</p><p><em>Create an internet gateway, and attach it to the VPC. Configure the private subnet route table to use the internet gateway as the default route</em> is incorrect because instances in private subnets cannot use an internet gateway directly unless they also have public IP addresses, which would make them publicly accessible.</p><p><em>Create a NAT instance, and place it in the same subnet where the EC2 instance is located. Configure the private subnet route table to use the NAT instance as the default route</em> is incorrect because NAT instances must reside in public subnets, not private ones. Otherwise, they cannot route traffic to the internet.</p><p><em>Create an internet gateway, and attach it to the VPC. Create a NAT instance, and place it in the same subnet where the EC2 instance is located. Configure the private subnet route table to use the internet gateway as the default route</em> is also incorrect. While it mixes correct and incorrect elements, it still places the NAT instance in the wrong subnet and misconfigures the route table. The internet gateway should not be the default route for private subnets.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your understanding of how to enable controlled outbound internet access from private subnets while maintaining secure and scalable architecture. Understanding NAT gateways and their placement is fundamental to VPC networking design.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Private subnets use NAT gateways or NAT instances in a public subnet for outbound internet access. The key is that only the NAT device is connected to the internet gateway, not the private instances themselves.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create a NAT gateway, and place it in a public subnet. Configure the private subnet route table to use the NAT gateway as the default route",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create an internet gateway, and attach it to the VPC. Configure the private subnet route table to use the internet gateway as the default route",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create a NAT instance, and place it in the same subnet where the EC2 instance is located. Configure the private subnet route table to use the NAT instance as the default route",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
