{
  "id": 252,
  "query": "<p>A legal services company is designing a storage system for managing sensitive client case files. These files are considered critical company assets and are expected to increase in volume over time. The storage solution must support access from multiple Amazon EC2-based application servers simultaneously. Additionally, the system should include built-in redundancy to ensure durability and high availability. What is the best solution to meet these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)</strong>.</p><p>Amazon EFS is a fully managed, scalable file storage service designed to be shared across multiple Amazon EC2 instances. It supports standard file system semantics, offers built-in redundancy across Availability Zones, and automatically grows as files are added. It is ideal for workloads that require shared access to file-based data and consistent low-latency performance across instances.</p><p><em>Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)</em> provides block storage for a single EC2 instance (or a set of instances using special configurations like multi-attach for io1/io2 volumes), but it is not designed for concurrent access by multiple EC2 instances in the same way as a shared file system. EBS is ideal for single-instance use cases like boot volumes or application data disks, not for shared multi-server access.</p><p><em>Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive</em> is a low-cost archival storage class optimized for infrequent access and long-term data retention. It is not suitable for active workloads or applications that require low-latency, file-level access across multiple servers.</p><p><em>AWS Backup</em> is a data protection service, not a primary storage solution. It’s useful for backing up existing storage resources like EFS, EBS, and RDS, but it does not provide direct access or redundancy for active file storage.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It evaluates your ability to choose an appropriate storage service based on access patterns, durability, and scalability needs. Understanding the differences between block, file, and archival storage is critical for designing high-performing AWS workloads.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When you see requirements involving shared access from multiple EC2 instances and scalability over time, Amazon EFS is usually the correct choice, especially if file-level access is needed.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Amazon Elastic File System (Amazon EFS)",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Amazon Elastic Block Store (Amazon EBS)",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "AWS Backup",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    }
  ],
  "objectives": []
},
{
  "id": 254,
  "query": "<p>A company has recently completed the migration of a three-tier web application to a new VPC in AWS. Upon review, the security team finds that the current Amazon EC2 security group configuration violates the principle of least privilege. Specifically, the ingress and egress rules between the web, application, and database tiers are overly permissive. What should a solutions architect recommend to enforce tighter security controls between tiers?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Create security group rules using the security group ID as the source or destination</strong>.</p><p>Using the security group ID as the source or destination in another security group rule ensures that only resources specifically associated with that security group can communicate. This approach enables granular control between tiers (e.g., only allow traffic from the application tier security group to the database tier), supporting the principle of least privilege while remaining scalable and dynamic.</p><p><em>Create security group rules using the instance ID as the source or destination</em> is incorrect because security groups do not support referencing EC2 instance IDs directly in rules. Rules must refer to IP addresses, CIDR blocks, or security group IDs.</p><p><em>Create security group rules using the VPC CIDR blocks as the source or destination</em> is too broad. This approach allows communication from any instance within the VPC, which does not satisfy least privilege requirements.</p><p><em>Create security group rules using the subnet CIDR blocks as the source or destination</em> offers more control than using the entire VPC CIDR, but it is still too permissive, especially in environments where multiple unrelated resources may reside in the same subnet.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It assesses your understanding of how to use security groups to enforce the principle of least privilege between application tiers in a cloud environment. Proper segmentation and access control are vital for minimizing risk and limiting potential attack vectors.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When isolating traffic between application layers (such as web → app → DB), referencing security group IDs is a best practice to ensure only the intended EC2 instances can communicate, without relying on IP-based rules that may become outdated or misconfigured.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create security group rules using the security group ID as the source or destination",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create security group rules using the instance ID as the source or destination",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create security group rules using the VPC CIDR blocks as the source or destination",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
      "text": "Create security group rules using the subnet CIDR blocks as the source or destination",
      "correct": false,
      "selected": false
