{
  "id": 259,
  "query": "<p>A company is updating its compliance policies and now requires long-term retention of backups for all databases running on Amazon RDS DB instances. The company must retain daily backups for a minimum period of 7 years. These backups must be consistent and restorable. What should a solutions architect recommend to meet these requirements?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Create a backup vault in AWS Backup to retain RDS backups. Create a new backup plan with a daily schedule and an expiration period of 7 years after creation. Assign the RDS DB instances to the backup plan</strong>.</p><p>AWS Backup provides a centralized, fully managed backup solution for AWS services, including Amazon RDS. With AWS Backup, you can create a custom backup plan that includes a daily backup schedule and set a retention period of 7 years. This ensures that backups are consistent, automatically managed, and restorable. It also supports compliance requirements through backup vaults and access controls.</p><p><em>Configure a backup window for the RDS DB instances for daily snapshots. Assign a snapshot retention policy of 7 years to each RDS DB instance. Use Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager (Amazon DLM) to schedule snapshot deletions</em> is incorrect because Amazon DLM is designed for Amazon EBS snapshots, not RDS snapshots. RDS snapshot retention must be managed through RDS or AWS Backup.</p><p><em>Configure database transaction logs to be automatically backed up to Amazon CloudWatch Logs with an expiration period of 7 years</em> is incorrect because CloudWatch Logs is intended for monitoring and observability—not for storing complete, restorable RDS backups.</p><p><em>Configure an AWS Database Migration Service (AWS DMS) replication task. Deploy a replication instance, and configure a change data capture (CDC) task to stream database changes to Amazon S3 as the target. Configure S3 Lifecycle policies to delete the snapshots after 7 years</em> is incorrect because DMS is used for migration and replication, not as a backup and restore solution. It does not provide point-in-time recovery or consistent snapshotting.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It assesses your knowledge of backup and compliance features in AWS, especially for RDS. Long-term data retention for audit and regulatory compliance is a critical requirement in many industries, and AWS Backup is the best tool for managing this in a scalable, policy-driven way.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When backup retention is tied to a specific time frame (like 7 years), and backups must be restorable, AWS Backup is often the correct service. Be careful not to confuse monitoring (CloudWatch), migration (DMS), or snapshot management tools (DLM) with full-featured backup solutions.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create a backup vault in AWS Backup to retain RDS backups. Create a new backup plan with a daily schedule and an expiration period of 7 years after creation. Assign the RDS DB instances to the backup plan",
