{
  "id": 261,
  "query": "<p>A global retail company has recently launched its ecommerce website to serve users around the world. The website is hosted on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB), and the instances are managed by an Auto Scaling group that spans multiple Availability Zones. The company wants to deliver tailored content to customers based on the type of device they are using to browse the website. What combination of actions should a solutions architect take to achieve this goal?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answers are <strong>Configure Amazon CloudFront to cache multiple versions of the content</strong> and <strong>Configure a Lambda@Edge function to send specific objects to users based on the User-Agent header</strong>.</p><p><strong>Configure Amazon CloudFront to cache multiple versions of the content</strong> enables the CDN to cache content variations based on request headers such as <code>User-Agent</code>. By including the appropriate headers in the cache key, CloudFront can store and deliver customized content for different device types (e.g., mobile vs. desktop).</p><p><strong>Configure a Lambda@Edge function to send specific objects to users based on the User-Agent header</strong> allows you to run lightweight serverless functions at AWS edge locations, customizing the behavior of CloudFront. You can inspect the <code>User-Agent</code> header and modify the request to serve device-specific content dynamically without changing the backend infrastructure.</p><p><em>Configure a host header in a Network Load Balancer to forward traffic to different instances</em> is incorrect because Network Load Balancers (NLBs) operate at Layer 4 (TCP/UDP) and do not support host-based routing or HTTP header inspection. That functionality is provided by Application Load Balancers (ALBs), not NLBs.</p><p><em>Configure AWS Global Accelerator. Forward requests to a Network Load Balancer (NLB). Configure the NLB to set up host-based routing to different EC2 instances</em> is also incorrect because, again, NLBs do not support host-based routing. Global Accelerator provides static IP addresses and routing optimization, but it does not support content-based routing logic.</p><p><em>Configure AWS Global Accelerator. Forward requests to a Network Load Balancer (NLB). Configure the NLB to set up path-based routing to different EC2 instances</em> is invalid because NLBs cannot perform path-based routing. This capability belongs to Application Load Balancers, and Global Accelerator alone cannot perform content inspection or routing decisions based on URL paths.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It evaluates your knowledge of how to serve dynamic, device-aware content using AWS services like CloudFront and Lambda@Edge. It also tests your understanding of the capabilities and limitations of AWS load balancing and edge services.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When tailoring content based on headers like <code>User-Agent</code>, think CloudFront + Lambda@Edge. Always remember that Network Load Balancers operate at a lower layer and lack content inspection and routing features.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Configure Amazon CloudFront to cache multiple versions of the content",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
