{
  "id": 262,
  "query": "<p>A company is deploying a multi-tier web application that uses Amazon ElastiCache to accelerate data retrieval. The solutions architect has set up two separate VPCs in the us-east-1 Region: one for the application layer (App VPC), which hosts EC2 instances, and another for the caching layer (Cache VPC), which runs the ElastiCache cluster. The architect must design a solution that enables secure communication between the EC2 instances and the ElastiCache cluster in the most cost-effective way. What is the best solution to implement?</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is <strong>Create a peering connection between the VPCs. Add a route table entry for the peering connection in both VPCs. Configure an inbound rule for the ElastiCache cluster’s security group to allow inbound connection from the application’s security group</strong>.</p><p>VPC peering is the most cost-effective solution for enabling communication between two VPCs in the same AWS Region. Once the peering connection is established, route table entries must be added to both VPCs to direct traffic across the peering connection. Additionally, security groups should be configured to allow the appropriate traffic. ElastiCache supports VPC peering, making this a fully compatible and cost-efficient design.</p><p><em>Create a Transit VPC. Update the VPC route tables in the Cache VPC and the App VPC to route traffic through the Transit VPC. Configure an inbound rule for the ElastiCache cluster's security group to allow inbound connection from the application’s security group</em> is incorrect because a Transit VPC adds significant complexity and cost. It is more suitable for hub-and-spoke network designs or multi-account environments with many VPCs, not just two VPCs in the same Region.</p><p><em>Create a peering connection between the VPCs. Add a route table entry for the peering connection in both VPCs. Configure an inbound rule for the peering connection’s security group to allow inbound connection from the application’s security group</em> is incorrect because VPC peering itself does not have a security group. Security group rules must reference other security groups or CIDR blocks within VPCs, not the peering connection.</p><p><em>Create a Transit VPC. Update the VPC route tables in the Cache VPC and the App VPC to route traffic through the Transit VPC. Configure an inbound rule for the Transit VPC’s security group to allow inbound connection from the application’s security group</em> is also incorrect for the same reasons as the previous Transit VPC option: it's costlier and unnecessary for this simple two-VPC setup.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your knowledge of inter-VPC connectivity options and cost-efficient network architecture. Choosing between VPC peering and Transit Gateway/Transit VPC solutions depends on scale, Region, and the number of VPCs involved.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> For two VPCs in the same Region, VPC peering is typically the best choice in terms of simplicity and cost—just remember to update the route tables and configure appropriate security group rules.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create a peering connection between the VPCs. Add a route table entry for the peering connection in both VPCs. Configure an inbound rule for the ElastiCache cluster’s security group to allow inbound connection from the application’s security group",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
    },
    {
