{
  "id": 263,
  "query": "<p>A company is developing an application composed of multiple microservices. To simplify deployment and operations, the company plans to use containers in the AWS Cloud. The solution must require minimal maintenance, automatically scale based on demand, and must not involve the company managing any infrastructure. Which combination of actions should a solutions architect take to meet these requirements? (Choose two.)</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answers are:<br><strong>1. Deploy an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) service with a Fargate launch type. Specify a desired task number level of greater than or equal to 2.</strong><br><strong>2. Deploy an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster.</strong></p><p>Amazon ECS with the Fargate launch type is a serverless option for running containers where AWS handles the provisioning, scaling, and maintenance of the infrastructure. By specifying a desired number of tasks (≥2), ECS ensures high availability and scales tasks across Availability Zones. ECS also provides service discovery, load balancing, and auto scaling natively.</p><p><em>Deploying the Kubernetes control plane on Amazon EC2 instances</em> and <em>Deploying Kubernetes worker nodes on Amazon EC2 instances</em> are incorrect because they require the company to manage and scale the infrastructure, violating the constraint that the company cannot manage additional infrastructure. These actions are associated with self-managed Kubernetes deployments, not managed services like Amazon EKS with Fargate.</p><p><em>Deploying an ECS service with the EC2 launch type</em> is also incorrect because it requires managing the underlying EC2 instances (cluster infrastructure), which adds operational overhead and contradicts the company's goal of minimizing maintenance and infrastructure management.</p><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It evaluates your ability to choose fully managed, scalable, and low-maintenance container deployment options on AWS.</p><p><strong>Tip:</strong> When infrastructure management is not allowed, always lean toward serverless or fully managed services like Fargate over EC2-based options.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Deploy an Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) cluster.",
      "correct": true,
      "selected": false
