{
  "id": 264,
  "query": "<p>A company operates a web application across 10 Amazon EC2 instances. Amazon Route 53 is used to direct traffic to the application. Occasionally, users encounter timeout errors when accessing the site. The networking team has determined that some DNS queries resolve to IP addresses of EC2 instances that are no longer healthy. What should a solutions architect do to prevent Route 53 from routing traffic to unhealthy instances and reduce timeout errors? (Choose 1)</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is:<br><strong>Create an Application Load Balancer (ALB) with a health check in front of the EC2 instances. Route to the ALB from Route 53.</strong></p><p>Using an Application Load Balancer (ALB) allows Route 53 to route all traffic to a single DNS name, while the ALB performs active health checks on the EC2 instances and only sends traffic to healthy ones. This is a scalable and fault-tolerant design that automatically avoids unhealthy instances without exposing them via DNS directly.</p><p><em>Why other options are incorrect:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Route 53 simple routing policy</strong> does not support health checks and distributes traffic randomly among all records.</li><li><strong>Route 53 failover routing policy</strong> is meant for active-passive configurations, not for distributing traffic among many active EC2 instances.</li><li><strong>Amazon CloudFront with EC2 as origin</strong> does not provide automatic health-based routing; it is meant for caching and content delivery, not backend health management.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your knowledge of combining Route 53 with Elastic Load Balancing for application resilience and health-aware traffic distribution.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Create a Route 53 simple routing policy record for each EC2 instance. Associate a health check with each record.",
      "correct": false,
