},
{
  "id": 265,
  "query": "<p>A solutions architect is designing a three-tier web application with web, application, and database layers. The application must be highly available and deliver HTTPS content with the lowest latency. Content should be served as close to the users as possible. Security is a top priority. What solution should the architect implement to meet these requirements? (Choose 1)</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is:<br><strong>Configure a public Application Load Balancer (ALB) with multiple redundant Amazon EC2 instances in private subnets. Configure Amazon CloudFront to deliver HTTPS content using the public ALB as the origin.</strong></p><p>This architecture ensures that the content is served securely and with low latency by using CloudFront at the edge, while keeping EC2 instances in private subnets enhances security. The ALB is public to allow CloudFront to access it, and CloudFront serves as the HTTPS endpoint to users, caching and distributing content globally.</p><p><em>Why the other options are incorrect:</em></p><ul><li><strong>Public EC2 instances in public subnets</strong> expose the application unnecessarily and are less secure.</li><li><strong>CloudFront directly accessing EC2 instances</strong> without an ALB is not scalable or recommended for redundancy.</li><li><strong>Using EC2 instances as CloudFront origins</strong> bypasses the load balancer, reducing high availability and scalability.</li></ul><p><strong>Why this question matters:</strong> It tests your ability to architect secure, performant web applications using edge caching (CloudFront) and private networking best practices.</p>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
