{
  "id": 266,
  "query": "<p>A company runs a globally distributed gaming platform on AWS. The application is latency-sensitive, as delays can affect gameplay and create unfair advantages. The platform is deployed in every AWS Region using Amazon EC2 instances in Auto Scaling groups behind Application Load Balancers (ALBs). A solutions architect must implement a solution to monitor health and redirect users to healthy endpoints with the lowest latency. What should the architect do? (Choose 1)</p>",
  "answer": "<p>The correct answer is:<br><strong>Configure an accelerator in AWS Global Accelerator. Add a listener for the port that the application listens on, and attach it to a Regional endpoint in each Region. Add the ALB as the endpoint.</strong></p><p><strong>Explanation:</strong> AWS Global Accelerator improves performance and availability by directing traffic to the closest healthy Regional endpoint using the AWS global network. It supports health checks and automatically reroutes traffic to healthy endpoints when failures occur, ensuring low-latency access for a global audience—critical for a gaming platform.</p><p><em>Why the other options are incorrect:</em></p><ul><li><strong>CloudFront with ALB origin:</strong> CloudFront is designed for static and cached content delivery. It is not ideal for low-latency, dynamic application workloads like online gaming.</li><li><strong>CloudFront with S3 origin:</strong> Using S3 is irrelevant in this case, as the origin needs to be the ALB serving the game engine, not static content.</li><li><strong>DynamoDB with DAX:</strong> This improves database latency, but it doesn’t address traffic routing or global health monitoring of endpoints.</li></ul>",
  "marked": 0,
  "timespent": 0,
  "options": [
    {
